## 2026-06-24 — trie-native data structure + direct evaluator + scaled benchmarks

Added src/main/scala/Trie.scala: a persistent, structurally-shared path-trie keyed by PathItem
(total order Symbol<Variable<Arity via `given pathItemOrdering`; TreeMap children; `terminal`
marks a path ending at a node). Canonical smart-constructor prunes empty subtries so `==` is
exact set-equality. All MORKL operations are native:
  - ring: union/intersection/subtraction (recursive merges, smallest-first probing).
  - prefix: composition GRAFTS b at each terminal of a, sharing the SAME b (O(#terminals) new
    nodes); wrap/unwrap walk a spine; restriction is a guided descent (keep subtree when a
    prefix ends), raffination = subtract(restriction).
  - tails: TailsUnion = joinAll(children); TailsIntersection = meetAll(children); head.
  - n-ary join-all / meet-all (the highlighted ops): joinAll groups all branches' children by
    key in ONE pass (O(total) vs reduce's re-merged accumulator); meetAll iterates the SMALLEST
    branch and probes the rest, recursing only surviving keys — bounded by the smallest branch
    (× k), not the largest. Measured 22x–118x over pairwise reduce on a tiny-among-large case.
  - First/Last sort by `.show` to match the reference; transformation crosses the SpaceValue
    boundary (rare). Conversions toPaths/fromPaths/fromSpaceValue/toSpaceValue + pathsInOrder.
  - Zipper (read): descend(k)/ascend/childKeys/isTerminal/path/root — the imperative-execution
    view (cf. PathMap) for a future RecursiveOpGraph-zipper backend.
Added evalT: a direct trie evaluator mirroring `eval`, with NATIVE `iter` (each child IS its
head's tail-trie — no flat-set regrouping) and the fixpoint-stabilization Call special-case;
grounded/residual/transform ops cross the SpaceValue boundary.

Tests (TrieTest.scala): TrieOps (6) — round-trip + every op agrees with `eval` over 1000+
random spaces, join-all/meet-all == pairwise reduce, zipper round-trip; TrieEval (6) — evalT ==
eval on aunt, semi-naive datalog, transitive, Game of Life (grounded), n-queens, sliding puzzle.
All green. Full suite (excluding Slow) **117 passing / 0 failing**.

Benchmarks (TrieBench.scala -> BENCHMARKS.md), eval (Set) vs evalT (trie), scaled:
  - datalog-TC chain: 2.1x -> 7.7x -> 13.6x (n=128: 1293ms -> 95ms). Win grows with size.
  - aunt-query synthetic family: 8.4x -> 19.7x (n=1600: 1623ms -> 82ms).
  - game-of-life random grids: up to 4.4x (32x32).
  - temperature (resident trie, prefix range query): 48x–927x (guided descent vs full scan).
  - meet-all microbench: 22x (k=40) -> 118x (k=120) — the non-trivial asymptotic.
  - sliding-puzzle (incl. full 3x3 = 181440) and n-queens (to n=11): 0.4–0.9x, HONESTLY neutral/
    slower because the work is in GROUNDED host functions (moves/constraints), so every step
    crosses the trie<->SpaceValue boundary; the backing structure isn't the bottleneck there.
Geometric mean 5.2x overall; 12.2x on the algebra-bound domains. SC×trie compose for datalog;
the aunt SC residual bloats (honest anti-result). Analysis written in BENCHMARKS.md.

Conclusion: the trie is a verified-exact, asymptotically-better backing store for the MORKL
algebra; it delivers large, size-growing speedups wherever the computation is expressed in the
algebra (joins/iteration/restriction/tails), and is neutral where the computation is delegated
to grounded host functions — which is itself a useful, honest finding.

