[2026-06-27] INCREMENT 6: ARG-CHANGING / DEFUNCTIONALIZED MUTUAL RECURSION (plan §5.5/§7.2 follow-up).
Two new lowerings:
 - asFixpointGeneral: lowers a self-recursion r(refs;mentions)=BASE ∪ r(refs; ONE mention transformed
   by T) for ARBITRARY base (not just identity, which is all asFixpoint handled) and arbitrary T, via a
   TWO-TAGGED-STATE Space.Fixpoint: state = #arg#·(current arg) ∪ #out#·(accumulated output); each step
   reads cur=Unwrap(state,#arg#), advances #arg#·T[cm:=cur] and emits #out#·BASE[cm:=cur]; the #out#
   projection of the lfp is ⋃ₖ BASE(Tᵏ(arg)) — the recursion's meaning under eval's iterate-and-
   accumulate Fixpoint semantics. Gated by structural monotonicity of BASE and T in the changing mention.
 - lowerMutualByElimination: lowers a 2-routine arg-changing mutual SCC by GAUSSIAN ELIMINATION —
   one-shot unfold (inline) one routine into the other (requires the unfolded one not to self-call) to
   obtain a single self-recursion, then asFixpointGeneral. lowerMutualSCC now = passthrough.orElse(elim).
NOTABLE: the lowered Fixpoint CONVERGES to the least fixpoint even where naive recursive eval DIVERGES
(e.g. f(∅)↔g(∅) loops forever under unfolding) — the core value of fixpoint lowering.
VERIFIED (GraphExecT): f(x)=x∪g(x("h")), g(x)=x∪f(x) lowers; eval==evalI==execT==⋃ₖ x("h"ᵏ)={h.h.a,h.a,a};
non-monotone f(x)=a\g(x),g(x)=b\f(x) correctly STAYS residual. No regressions: non-Slow 162/0;
