4. SYMBOLIC COST (review.md 3) — SpatialCost.scala
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Cardinality is NOT cost, and this file is built around that. Cardinality/length/head-count are the
INPUTS (`Meas`); the output is a Cost VECTOR per node — work (elementary visits), alloc (paths or
trie nodes materialised), rounds (head-groups and fixpoint rounds entered) — over `Sym`
(Const|Var|Add|Mul|Pow|Max|Log|Inf) with a real polynomial normal form. Vars range over reals ≥ 2,
which is what makes the syntactic `dominates` sound (monomial matching + a log₂x ≤ x trade — that is
what derives N² ≥ N log N). `BigO(expFactors, degree, logs)` compared lexicographically IS
2^N > N² > N log N > N > log N > 1, so the distinctions review.md says collapse to one INF stay
separate. TWO backends consume the SAME facts and disagree, which is what makes it a cost model:
  unwrap     set work=n         trie work=1   (zipper focus, no rebuild)
  range      set work=n² log n  trie work=n
  restrict   set work=n³        trie work=n²
  union      set work=n         trie work=n²
MEASURED (SpatialCostCheck, 20 tests): dominates sound on 114 pairs x 60 valuations = 6840 points;
monotonicity on 48 cost components and under a bigger DECLARED input type; 300 corpus programs x 2
backends in 1258 ms, 600/600 work components BOUNDED, 0 reach `inf`; the two backends disagree on
300/300 programs; set-backend order classes spread over n .. n⁹ log n.
*** The per-operator constants are a MODEL of the two interpreters, read off eval and the trie/zipper
ops. They are NOT measured constants and NOT proved. The only evidence they track reality is a WEAK
rank correlation of set-backend work against measured eval runtime: Spearman ρ = 0.933 over 16
programs. That is a sanity check. Do not cite it as a validated cost model. ***

