1. THE ONE FAILURE, AND WHY IT WAS NOT A REGRESSION  (SpatialRecursionCheck)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
One suite came back red: SpatialRecursionCheck "NO BOUND: M1 holds but the NUMERIC drop (M2) does
not". The test asserted `bounded == None` for

    peel(p0, m) = heads(m) u peel(p0, Unwrap(m, p0)),   |p0| in [1,3],   maxLen(m) = 4

on the reasoning that the histogram's variable-length Unwrap arm loses the length bound, so M2
cannot be shown. A bound WAS derived. I did not accept "stale expectation" as an answer; I
diagnosed it component by component before touching the test.

WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS (measured, each line a printed observation):
  * the histogram alone STILL loses it: SpatialTypes.infer(Unwrap(m,p0)) with |p0| in [1,3] returns
    len = [0, INF]. The test's premise about the histogram was and remains TRUE.
  * the histogram is no longer the only component asked. keyType(lengthAnnotation(1,4)) reduces
    Shape.top to a depth-<=4 trie (reducer rule H3, histogram -> shape), giving shape.lens = [1,4].
  * Shape.unwrapUnknown then shifts that trie down by |p0|.lo = 1 level. Sound: Unwrap(s,p) with
    |p| = j is a subset of the level-j tail-sets, i.e. tailsUnion iterated j times; the transfer
    unions that over j in [k.lo, k.hi] and widens to weaken(top) when k.hi > MaxDepth + 2.
  * the product's `len` meet reads the bound back off the shape: [0,3]. So M2 is PROVED, not skipped.
  * the drop is always the LOWER end of |p0|: |p0| in [2,3] gives mu chain 4->2->0 and depth 2.

SOUNDNESS EVIDENCE, three independent ways:
  * Shape.unwrapUnknown is already differentially gated by SpatialSoundnessHunt's randomised
    shape-transfer sweep (case 15, randLen containing the concrete prefix length) — green.
  * eval vs the residual, INSIDE the precondition, over five |p0| intervals: 7,500 conforming
    inputs, 0 disagreements. My first attempt at this generator produced paths of length 5 and
    "found" 2 disagreements — the generator had left the annotation, not the residual; the suite now
    asserts SpatialTyping.accepts on every generated input before comparing, so that cannot recur.
  * the guard is still load-bearing: |p0| in [0,3] (a prefix that may be EMPTY, so nothing drops)
    still returns NoBound with the M1 reason. Manufacturing a bound there would be the real bug.
  * the MaxDepth cap is NOT being misread as a length claim: Shape.top.lens.hi = INF and
    SpatialType.top admits a path 3x deeper than MaxDepth. Both asserted.

RESOLUTION: the test was REWRITTEN, not ignored, to assert the sound new behaviour plus all four
checks above, and renamed "M2 over a VARIABLE-length prefix: the reduced product proves the numeric
drop". Its docstring records the old expectation and why it changed. SpatialAnalysisCheck already
carried the same observation from the other side ("REDUCER x RECURSION ... (was NoBound: 'M2
failed')", 300 admitted inputs, 0 disagreements), so two suites now pin it from two directions.
NOTHING WAS MARKED .ignore IN THIS INCREMENT.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE REDUCED PRODUCT IS NOW BIDIRECTIONAL  (finding 5)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`SpatialType.reduce` was ONE mutation (the shape's total capping each histogram upper). It is now
EIGHT named rules iterated to a fixed point under SpatialConfig.reduceRounds:
  H1..H4  histogram -> shape : per-depth counts bound a fibre's untracked-head room; zero/positive
          support forces eps No/Must; "nothing longer than L" prunes the untracked branches too,
          MATERIALISING an otherTail where there was none; unique possible location is forced.
  S1..S4  shape -> histogram : implied total caps each class; classes outside the shape's length
          hull die; a shape-forced class gets a lower bound; an empty meet is a CONTRADICTION.
A contradiction does NOT widen — it collapses to the explicit SpatialType.bottom, whose gamma is
empty (it rejects even the empty space), which is what lets a caller tell "the value is empty" from
"your annotations are unsatisfiable".

MEASURED (SpatialAnalysisCheck, 19 tests):
  * reducer terminates in <= 1 round on 225 generated types (cap 4); non-idempotent 0.
  * reduce gamma-gate: 225 types x 128 values = 28,800 checks, 529 members; tightened 71 types;
    MEMBERS LOST 0.
  * meet gamma-gate: 48^2 pairs x 128 values = 294,912 checks; meet was bottom on 2,256 pairs;
    members lost 0; FALSE BOTTOM 0.
  * per-node: the visitor's return value REPLACES the node's shape, so the parent transfer consumes
    the tightened child — that is the part a root-only reduction cannot do. 400 corpus instances,
    10,227 decorated nodes, differs from `infer` on 46, bottom on 0, root <= infer on all.
  * 4,352 closed decorated occurrences checked against eval: violations 0.

*** HONEST SCOPE. reduce has NO SMT obligation — nothing in proofs/spatial{,-semantic} covers it,
and it is now load-bearing for a call-depth bound (section 1). Termination is MEASURED (<=1 round on
a generated pool) plus a hard round cap, not argued: there is no proof the eight rules form a
decreasing chain. Also: the product is mutual AT THE REDUCTION STEP, not inside the transfers.
SpatialTypes.infer still computes counts without consulting the shape and Shape's transfers compute
structure without consulting counts; they meet only after each node. peel works because
correction-after-the-node suffices there, not because the transfers cooperate. ***

