2. THE ONE REAL INTEGRATION FAILURE, AND THE FIX
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SpatialCheckCheck failed 4 of 25 (4a, 4b, 4c, 6b) on the merged tree. It is not in the four agents'
file sets, so nobody ran it; both failures are consequences of the order-completeness work.

(a) 6b, "the order mirror agrees with `leq` on every pair" — A GENUINE DEFECT, now fixed.
`SpatialChannels.histOrderFailures` (SpatialCheck.scala) RE-STATED `leqSpace`'s clauses in order to
attribute a rejection to a channel. When `leqSpace` was completed for the spill-vs-tracked partition
(canonSpace + the window decomposition in `leqSpaceMask`), the mirror kept rejecting pairs the order
now proves, and 6b caught it: `leq=true, channels=length class 1, spill length window, spill
aggregate count`. A duplicated order in the diagnosis layer is exactly how this drifts, so the fix
removes the duplication rather than resynchronising it: `histOrderFailures` now takes the VERDICT
from `SpatialGamma.leqSpaceMask` itself, uses the set bits to select which channels to report, and
quotes the CANONICAL forms the order actually compared. A mask of 0 returns no failures by
construction. A set bit that the per-length loop cannot localise still yields one generic failure, so
a rejected pair can never come back with an empty explanation (4d gates that).
  MEASURED AFTER: the mirror agrees with `leq` on 2000 of 2000 pairs (1855 rejected, each with at
  least one named channel). Note the verdict path in `SpatialCheck.types` branches on
  `SpatialType.leq` and never on `failures.isEmpty`, so this change moves DIAGNOSIS only, never a
  Proved/Refuted/Unknown decision.

(b) 4a/4b/4c, "the false negative is REAL" — THE TEST WAS ASSERTING A REPAIRED BUG.
Its witness was `{; other lens [1,2]: [1,1]} ⊑ {len 1: [0,1]; len 2: [0,1]}`, which is literally one
of the two pairs SpatialLawCheck now prints as `REPAIRED`. I re-pointed the three tests instead of
re-breaking the order. First I MEASURED whether any false negative survives for the type the old
routine inferred: over a 174-type pool, ZERO — and zero for every point type alpha(v), v in U. The
surviving class is PRODUCT INTERACTION, so 4a-4c now pin an instance of it:

    a = shape {eps!, a.{eps!}, b.{eps!}}  lens {; other lens [0,1]: [3,3]}
    b = shape {eps!, a.{eps!}, b.{eps!}}  lens {len 0: [1,1]; len 1: [2,2]}

Both carry the same shape, which pins the value to exactly {eps; a; b}, so gamma(a) = gamma(b) = one
space and containment holds OUTRIGHT, not merely on a finite universe (asserted: each admits exactly
one member of U, and that member is the same). The histogram halves are not in the order either way
(a.lens admits the count vector "0 at length 0, 3 at length 1", which b.lens rejects); the shape
halves are EQUAL. So the containment is visible only to the conjunction, and no componentwise order
-- and `SpatialType.leq` is `Shape.leqStrong x leqSpace` -- can see it. This one is NOT repairable
inside `leqSpace`; the previous occupant of these tests was, and was repaired.
  4c now reaches it through a real routine the honest way: `routine(s) = s` over a declared mention
  infers its input's type exactly, so `SpatialCheck.report` sees the same pair. The assumption it is
  Unknown MODULO changes from InputPathAnnotation to InputSpaceAnnotation; 5-series coverage of
  InputPathAnnotation is unaffected (it is emitted at SpatialCheckCheck.scala:510 as well).

(c) ONE SEMANTIC ADDITION, in `orderFailures`, because 4b's contract demanded an honest answer:
when ONE component of the product order is PROVED contained and the other is not, every clause from
the failing component is marked `sufficientOnly` and says why. That is a true statement, not a
convenience: the contained component may exclude exactly the values the other's clause objects to.
It is also the difference between the checker saying "the bounded universe cannot see a witness, so
nothing is claimed" and "this is the order's documented incompleteness, not a violation" -- and on a
pair where gamma(a) = gamma(b) the second is the correct sentence.

