================================================================================================
SESSION 2026-08-08 (cont) — THE N-ARY TRIE OPERATIONS WERE ASYMPTOTICALLY WRONG ON WIDE OPERANDS
================================================================================================
Steer: "The primary goal should be to improve the asymptotics of the trie operations." followed by
"Different data distributions will have different asymptotics: this is expected ... capture this,
and drive it down.  No over-approximations."

TWO GROWTH-CLASS DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED in the executing backend (`evalI` over `ITrie`):

1. `ITrie.joinAll`'s children step grouped every child of every operand into a LongMap of buffers and
   rebuilt the result map key by key: Theta(sum of fan) UNCONDITIONALLY.  The pairwise Patricia union
   it was introduced to beat answers non-interleaving operands in O(1).  So the n-ary pass was WORSE
   than the fold for few/wide operands -- a TailsUnion over 3 wide head-groups.
2. `ITrie.meetAll`'s children step iterated the smallest node's keys and probed the other k-1 maps:
   Theta(fan) probes where a Patricia prefix comparison rejects the pair outright.

FIX: `IntTrieOps.joinAllTries` / `meetAllTries` -- true n-ary simultaneous Patricia descents.  A
branch with one live operand is that operand BY POINTER; opposite sides of a branching bit means
disjoint regions (empty meet); k==2 delegates to the proven pairwise descent; reconstruction is
pointer-preserving so the n-ary ops can now conclude Identity, which the old ones could not (both
ended in an unconditional fresh node).

A FOLD IS NOT THE FIX, and OptimalTrieCheck's before/after gate caught the attempt: a pairwise fold
walks the first two operands' shared structure in full before a small third operand can prune it,
which loses the smallest-branch-at-every-level property.  The n-ary descent keeps both properties.

MEASURED (steps = ITrie/Patricia descents + per-key entries; OptimalTrieCheck DISTRIBUTION SWEEP):
  distribution   union/inter/sub        joinAll over 3        class
  separated      2 flat                 5 flat                O(1)
  adjacent       2 flat                 5 flat                O(1)
  offset-block   3 flat                 -                     O(1)
  striped        192..3072  slope 1.00  384..6144 slope 1.00   Theta(n), inherent
  half-paired    194..3074, 6.0 steps per paired key           Theta(paired)
  equal          384..6144, 6.0 steps per paired key           Theta(paired)
  joinAll of 3 WIDE operands: 16..24 (maxslope 0.30) against the group-by-key pass at 193..3073
  (slope 1.00) -- 128x at n=1024.  meetAll of 3 wide operands: 2 flat against 129..2049.
  absorbed joinAll / contained meetAll: 0 alloc, operand returned by pointer (was impossible before).

TWO OF MY OWN OVER-APPROXIMATIONS, REFUTED BY THE SWEEP AND RELABELLED: "adjacent" and
"offset-block" were labelled O(log n); both measure constant.  The honest statement is that
NON-INTERLEAVING key sets merge in O(1) however they are offset.

ALSO: `Trie.range` (TreeMap backend) had a DOCUMENTED degeneracy -- a full recursive size walk before
the identity check and a window rebuilt path-by-path through singleton+union.  Ported ITrie's
order-statistic slice: cached per-node terminal count (so a full window is the identity in O(1)) and
whole-subtrie accept/reject by pointer.  TreeMap already iterates in canonical order, so no memoised
child order is needed here.

A GENERATOR BUG IN MY OWN GATE, found by the gate: the distributions were expressed in SYMBOL names,
but `Interner` assigns ids in first-use order, so name-striping produced two ADJACENT id blocks and
the sweep measured the same layout twice ("striped" read flat, which is impossible).  The sweep now
interns one consecutive key pool and every distribution is an index set into it, asserted consecutive.

DOCS: docs/atlas.md rewritten as a compact index (1174 -> 357 lines): one row per component with its
file and one line of role, review.md references and rationale essays removed, stale line-number
anchors dropped in favour of greppable symbol names.  docs/ALGEBRA.md NOT touched (it is the theory
paper and does not describe the implementation) -- confirmed by `git diff`.
