--- WORKFLOW POST-MORTEM: two worktree agents on a base that did not exist
Ran a 2-agent workflow (wf_9cd73d15-a5a) racing the two tails remedies in isolated worktrees.  Route 1
finished; route 2 ran ~2h10m and was stopped.  THE CAUSE WAS NOT TIMEOUTS.
1. `isolation: worktree` branched BOTH worktrees from master (8cd36b9), which does not even contain
   SpatialCost.scala.  Route 1 detected it and `git reset --hard 344f694` onto branch b's tip -- but this
   session's four rounds of work are UNCOMMITTED, and worktree isolation cannot see uncommitted work.  So
   both agents measured a baseline of 62 requirement failures (the pre-session state) while the brief said
   51, and route 2 kept iterating trying to reconcile numbers that could never match.
2. ONE shared bloop server: 19.5h old, 9.4GB RSS, serving the main tree and both worktrees -- three
   project graphs in one 6GB heap.
3. The Bash 10-minute ceiling kills the CLIENT, not the server.  An overrunning `scala-cli test` leaves
   bloop executing; the agent retries; the new request queues behind the orphan.  That is the stacking.
AVOIDANCE: commit (or do not isolate) before spawning worktree agents; pin the base commit and have the
agent `git log -1` and ABORT in the first minute on a mismatch; make the agent report the BASELINE numbers
before editing anything, so a base mismatch surfaces at minute one; give each agent its own compiler
(`--server=false` or a per-worktree daemon), never N worktrees on one server; and background anything over
~2 minutes instead of a foreground call that gets killed mid-flight.
SALVAGED, AND IT ANSWERS THE QUESTION THE RACE WAS FOR.  Route 1 implemented monus as a FUNCTION in
`object Sym`, not an enum case, leaving `dominates` untouched -- the conservative option -- with
containment 100.00% on 3000 corpus and 59 cornerstone points.  Its numbers pin the real blocker: alloc
74 -> 66 (upper 73 -> 65) and touch 221 -> 197 / 1754 -> 1562, i.e. 8 off alloc and 24 = 3x8 off touch, so
