## 2026-08-31 — acceptance-review resolution pass, round 3: the analysis becomes a rewrite consumer, and it
## immediately finds a soundness bug in the frontier

ITEM 14 — `rewriteNodes` READ TWO THINGS.  A census of the consumers was blunt: of the eleven `Fact`
cases the analysis emits at every node, exactly ONE (`ExactHeadSet`) was read anywhere outside
`SpatialTypeSystem`, and that read was inside a Trie-backend-only candidate.  `DefinitelyNonEmpty`,
`MinimumCardinality`, `MaximumCardinality`, `AllPathsHaveAtLeast`, `MaximumPathLength`,
`HeadSetWithin`, `MinimumHeadCount`, `MaximumHeadCount` and `PrefixAbsent` had NO consumer at all —
`PrefixAbsent` was manufactured at every node and read nowhere — and neither did the whole relational
layer: `SpatialFrontier` priced a node, never replaced it.  Five rewrites now consume them:

  R1 frontierEdits  the relational frontier's SINGLETON case sets.  `{Empty}` / `{Left}` / `{Right}` /
                    `{Left,Right}` are proofs, not estimates, and each replaces the node by `∅` or by
                    one of its own operands.  Deliberately NOT `FrontierCase.isIdentity`, which is
                    true of `{Empty, Left}` — "∅ or L" is a disjunction, not a rewrite.
  R2 iterationEdits a source with `ExactHeadSet` of size 1 makes the loop a SUBSTITUTION
                    (`body[sym := h][rest := TailsUnion(src)]`), gated on hygiene and on the result
                    not being larger than the loop it replaces.
  R3 iterationEdits `MaximumHeadCount(0)` on the source, or a provably-empty body, gives `∅`.
  R4 prefixEdits    `PrefixAbsent(p)` makes `Unwrap(src, p)` `∅` — the fact's first consumer.
  R5 windowEdits    a pinned cardinality (`MinimumCardinality(k)` = `MaximumCardinality(k)`) whose
                    normalised window covers `[0, k)` makes `Range` the identity.

AND R1 IMMEDIATELY REFUTED THE FRONTIER.  Turning a COST verdict into a SEMANTIC rewrite is a much
stronger test of it, and the corpus differential found the hole on the first run:
`Restriction(x, x)` with `x = {ε}` was rewritten to `∅` where `eval` gives `{ε}`.  Root cause,
`SpatialFrontier.headDisjoint`: it proves the two HEAD SETS are disjoint and says nothing about ε,
which has no head — and ε is exactly the member these operators treat specially.  Three arms leaned
on it without an ε side-condition:

  Intersection   `headDisjoint ⇒ {Empty}` is wrong when BOTH sides may hold ε (ε survives the meet)
  Subtraction    `headDisjoint ⇒ {Left}`  is wrong when both may hold ε (ε is removed)
  Restriction    `headDisjoint ⇒ {Empty}` is wrong when the RIGHT may hold ε — ε is a prefix of
                 every path, so ALL of x survives however disjoint the heads
  Raffination    the dual: `headDisjoint ⇒ {Left}` is wrong when the right may hold ε (all of x goes)

Each arm now carries its own ε condition.  As a COST claim the same arms were predicting zero
allocation where a node is allocated, so this is a soundness fix for the cost model too, not only
for the new rewrite.

AND A MISSING OPTIMISATION, FOUND THE SAME WAY.  With R1 live, `SpatialPipelineCheck`'s hook contract
went red on a program where the hook made things WORSE by 2.54x predicted work.  Diagnosis:
`Iteration(src, h, t, Union(inv, dead))` was being hoisted by `Lower.IterUnion_Indep` into
`headed(src) · inv` — the invariant branch computed ONCE.  The spatial tier proved `dead` empty, the
union collapsed, and with no union left the hoist rule no longer matched: the loop came back and
`inv` was recomputed per head.  `docs/design_plan.md` §5.1 lists the missing rule as a "subsumed
special case" — it had never been written.  `Lower.IterInvariant_Drop` is it:
`iter(src, body) = headed(src) · body` for a body invariant in both binders, bare when
`provablyHeaded(src)`.  It must refuse a body that is provably `⊆ {ε}`, which is `headedGuard`'s own
shape; the first draft tested `body == Singleton(ε)` syntactically and HUNG, because another rule in
the same `all_forever` round normalises that to `Literal({ε})` and the refusal stopped matching.
Over 400 corpus programs the rule takes total predicted trie work from 4.36e6 to 3.78e6.

AND THE HOOK'S CONTRACT WAS MEASURING THE WRONG THING.  It was "the hooked body must not have more
NODES", and a guarded hoist legitimately trades ~12 nodes of guard for a branch that stops being
recomputed per head — the old assertion called `docs/design_plan.md` §5.1's headline optimisation a
regression.  The contract is now the PREDICTED COST, in two halves: the hook must lower the TOTAL
predicted work over the corpus (it does: 3011427 -> 2757123, 8.4% better over 250 programs), and no
single program may regress by more than 1.5x on any component (worst measured: 1.24x).  Both forms
are priced on the same facts with the same backend.  Final: 6250 differential checks against `eval`,
132 of 250 programs changed, nodes 10580 -> 9165.

ITEM 2 — THE RECURSION CERTIFICATES (agent report, verified).  `terminating/` went from 11 files /
44 goals proving that particular recurrences STOP, to 20 files / 157 goals that also say what
`Space.Fixpoint` COMPUTES and what each lowering pass PRESERVES: `fixpoint_is_lfp` (the accumulator
`A_n` IS `lfp_{⊇I}F` under monotonicity, and the executors return `A_n`, not `C_n`),
`asfixpoint_sound`, `unroll_vs_kleene` (with two machine-checked counterexamples showing the
monotonicity hypothesis is load-bearing), `seminaive_correct`, the tagged-state projection and
Bekić decomposition for mutual SCC lowering, and the bounded-recursion residual cut.
`terminating/REGISTRY.tsv` is a TOTAL map from the eleven lowering sites to their obligations, with
OPEN and PROPERTY rows kept visible rather than omitted; `terminating/STATUS.tsv` carries both
provers' verdicts.  `sh terminating/run.sh`: certified 20, expected-open 0, countermodels 0,
unexpected-open 0, 4m13s — and it now runs VAMPIRE PER GOAL on the reconstructed incremental context,
because vampire has no push/pop and would otherwise "prove" a staged file from one goal's refutation.

The live failure it was asked to fix — `datalog_b_seminaive_lemmas` FAIL (unsat 2/4, bad 1) — was
diagnosed rather than papered over: in INCREMENTAL (push/pop) mode z3 5.1.0 disables the macro
finder, so `allp`/`deltap` stay uninterpreted and the extensionality instance is never built.  Fixed
with three staging lemmas, each proved as its own goal first; `stalled_delta_empty` is unchanged in
statement.  60 s timeout -> 0.193 s for all seven goals.

AND IT REFUTED TWO ARMS OF THE MONOTONICITY GATE.  `mono` (MORKL.scala) and its mention form `monoIn`
both claimed `TailsIntersection` and `Iteration` are monotone in their operands.  Neither is:
  * `TailsIntersection` INTERSECTS the per-head tail sets, so a path with a NEW head adds a
    participant and can only SHRINK the result — `{0·7} ⊆ {0·7,1·8}` but `{7} ⊄ ∅`;
  * `Iteration` is monotone in its SOURCE only if the body is monotone in the TAIL-SET it binds, since
    a bigger source ENLARGES an existing group as well as adding groups — with body `{ε} ∖ ⋁(nbs<|{9})`,
    `{0·7} ⊆ {0·7,0·9}` but `{ε} ⊄ ∅`.
Both arms are now the conservative test (the SCC call / the mention is forbidden in the position).
No corpus or cornerstone program puts a recursive mention there, so this closes a missing guard
rather than fixing a wrong output — and `AgnosticPipeline.monotoneInMention` already got both right,
which is what makes the divergence a transcription bug rather than a disagreement.
