================================================================================================
ROUND 5 — `Lower.Iter_Tails` joins the ordinary lowering, and the crossover it exposes
================================================================================================

THE RULE.  `Iteration(src, _, rest, Mention(rest))` is `TailsUnion(src)`: `eval`'s Iteration groups
`src` by head DROPPING the headless path, binds `rest` to each group's tail set and unions the
bodies, which with the bare rest-mention as the body is `{ tail : h.tail in src }`; `eval`'s
TailsUnion is `recs(src).collect { case PathValue(_::r) => PathValue(r) }`, which drops the headless
path for the same reason and yields the same set.  On the trie side `ITrie.tailsUnion(s)` is
`joinAll(s.children.values)` and `s.children` likewise ignores `s.terminal`.  Certified as
`iter-tails` in the law registry (proofs/laws/law_tailsu_set.smt2 + proofs/keyfolds.smt2).  It was
reachable only from the supercompiler, so `Routine.optimized` — the body an executor is actually
handed — kept the loop, a binder and a head-group split and an n-ary accumulate, where every backend
has a direct `tailsUnion` entry point.  Nothing in the source said why, and nothing was relying on
the loop surviving.

THE ONE THING THAT WAS RELYING ON IT was `SpatialCostCheck`'s per-operator width table, whose
`iteration` row was EXACTLY this term.  With the rule in `OrdinaryRules` that row prices a
`TailsUnion` under the name `iteration` and duplicates the row four lines above it, so the row moved
in the same change.  A BODY THAT IS A FUNCTION OF THE TAIL-SET ALONE IS THE GENERAL FORM OF THAT
MISTAKE and not a special case: `U_h f(T_h) = f(U_h T_h)` for every `f` that distributes over union,
which is every operator here in its left argument, so `Subtraction(rest, B)`, `Restriction(rest, B)`
and `TailsUnion(rest)` are all collapsible in principle.  The new subject is
`Iteration(a, h, t, Wrap(Subtraction(t, b), Deref(h)))`: it binds BOTH names and TAGS its output with
the group head, so the grouping is load-bearing, and it is the shape every cornerstone loop actually
has (aunt, gol/nextStep, nqueens.place all bind the head and re-tag by it).  It also keeps the
per-group result LARGE, so `collectJoin` is still exercised on eight substantial operands.

WIDTH GATE 28 -> 24, AND FOUR OF THOSE FOUR ROWS ARE THE SUBJECT CHANGE, NOT A MODEL IMPROVEMENT.
Said out loud because the two are not the same thing and the ledger now says so at OP-1/OP-1g/OP-1z:
the new subject's `Touch` reads 34.78 (trie) / 36.82 (graph), inside the budget, and its `Work` and
`Alloc` are still red under OP-2/OP-6.  OP-3g is RETIRED — `execT`'s `Work` meets the SELECTION tier
on every operator of the table — and it is retired as a COMMENT and not as an empty `Limitation`,
because an entry with no measured subject is itself a ledger failure and the gate says so
("declared over <nothing> but NO requirement check produced a matching row").

IT IS NOT A WIN ON EVERY EXECUTABLE.  Interleaved A/B, best of 7 batches, 4096 paths shaped
`h(i%k).m(i%16).t(i)` so the k head children SHARE their `m` keys, sweeping k:

    k        2      4      8     16     32     64    128
    eval  5.27x  5.75x  5.24x  5.02x  5.75x  6.13x  6.00x
    evalI 4.97x  1.38x  1.43x  2.38x  1.08x  1.02x  1.02x
    execZ 0.77x  0.75x  1.06x  1.49x  0.13x  0.74x  1.13x
    execT 1.73x  1.33x  0.95x  1.05x  0.91x  0.32x  0.35x

`eval` and `evalI` win at every width.  `execT` LOSES BY 3x ON A WIDE SOURCE and the counted events
say exactly why: at k = 64 the loop costs `work 195 / alloc 113` and the TailsUnion costs
`work 34473 / alloc 25522`, ALL of the difference being `NaryOperandProbe` (34469) and
`NaryScratchSlot` (25504).  `GraphExec.scala`'s `case "Iteration"` accumulates with a PAIRWISE
`ITrie.union` left fold and never enters `ITrie.joinAll`; `ITrie.tailsUnion` IS `joinAll`, whose
`collectLive` and per-recursive-call `live`/`ls`/`rs` arrays are Theta(k) at EVERY NODE of the
Patricia recursion, and when the operands share their key space that recursion visits the whole
shared structure with all k operands live.  So the regression is a property of `joinAll` at high
arity over key-OVERLAPPING operands and NOT of this rewrite: the same `TailsUnion(a)` term is 3x
slower on `execT` than the loop it replaced.  `evalI`'s own Iteration uses `joinAll` too, which is
why that backend is a wash rather than a loss.  The cost model PREDICTED this — `naryProbes` and
`naryScratch` are the terms, and they are the same terms that make the `tails-*` Work rows red — so
the model is not what needs fixing.

THE RULE IS KEPT.  Every real source in this repository is narrow (the cornerstones' head counts are
single digits, where the row reads 0.95x-1.73x), it is a 5-6x win on the reference evaluator at every
width, and the term is strictly smaller.  Measured on the corpus: the differential form's node count
went 10580 -> 10549 and the hooked form's 9165 -> 9140 over the same 250 programs, i.e. the rule
fires on roughly one program in eight, and all 6250 differential checks against `eval` still pass.
The crossover is recorded as a DEFECT IN `ITrie.joinAll` — scratch arrays reallocated at every node
of the recursion — with the reproduction and the gate to add (`OptimalTrieCheck`'s arity ladder
covers key-DISJOINT operands and not key-overlapping ones).
