================================================================================================
ROUND 6 — tightening the remaining width rows, six clusters, every patch adversarially refuted
================================================================================================

HOW IT WAS RUN.  Six analysts, one per cluster of the 24 remaining width failures, each in a private
copy of `src/main/scala` with a shared harness that prints, for every (operator, backend, component)
of `SpatialCostCheck`'s fixture, the predicted interval AND the counted value from a warm run of that
executable on the same inputs — so a proposal that excludes the run says so on its own table.  Each
patch then went to an adversarial refuter whose default verdict was UNSOUND and whose brief was five
named attacks (the MAY/MUST trap, the fast-path enumeration, the lazy-val trap, the data distribution
that makes a removed term real, and BUILD FIXTURES AND RUN IT).  Four of the six were refuted, three
of those with a tested minimal repair.  THAT PHASE IS WHY THIS ROUND IS NOT A REGRESSION: every one
of the four refutations was a bound that passed the shipped fixture's own table and the 200-program
corpus, and failed only on a fixture built to break it.

WHAT LANDED, AND WHAT EACH ONE COST TO GET RIGHT.

  composition — SOUND as proposed, the only one of six.  `ITrie.compositionR`'s single allocation
  site is reached at exactly the LEFT operand's INTERIOR nodes: a LEAF terminal takes `rIdent(RIGHT)`
  and grafts `b` BY POINTER, allocating nothing.  So the floor is `I(a)`, not `N(a)` — and the arm
  says why the obvious `N(a)` is not merely loose but wrong (73 against a counted 9).
  `SpatialFacts.interiorNodes` derives `I_d >= K_d.lo - E_d.hi + E_{d+1}.lo` from the existing prefix
  profile with every input read in its weakening direction.  trie/graph `Alloc` 75.00 -> 7.50 with the
  floor EQUAL to the counted 9; zipper `Alloc` 542.00 -> 7.32 and `Work` 1131.50 -> 20.30.

  fixpoint — REFUTED, then landed in two thirds.  Two upper-endpoint corrections are right and are in:
  the accumulating merge runs `R - 1` times and not `R` (the accumulate sits in the `else` branch of
  `while !stop`, verified verbatim in all three loops), and round 1 is priced with `rec` bound to the
  SEED, because all three loops open `var cur = <init>` and evaluate the body first.  `Alloc`
  205/69/205 -> 46/23.50/46, `Touch` 1116 -> 691.  WHAT DID NOT GO IN is the LOWER endpoint that came
  with the seed pricing: `CostInterval.meet` joins the lower endpoints, which installs `priced`'s
  must-paired count as the fixpoint's floor at exactly the round where `var cur = evalI(init)` IS the
  caller's own trie by pointer and the body's sibling operand commonly reaches the same objects (the
  semi-naive Datalog shape).  Measured by the refuter on
  `Fixpoint(a, r, Union(r, Restriction(a, {h0})))` with `a` declared exactly: the joined floor claims
  `touch >= 11` (1 root + 1 paired head + 8 paired depth-2 prefixes + 1 entry) against a counted 4,
  and nine rows over four fixpoint fixtures went from inside their intervals to outside.  The repair
  is the upper-only meet `CostInterval(cb.lo, Cost.meetHi(raw.hi, cb.hi))` — the pattern `analyze`
  already uses for the zipper demand price — which keeps both upper wins and drops the width claim:
  `Touch` 558.50 -> 346.00, still red.  IT ALSO FOUND A PRE-EXISTING HOLE: `mustDescend` discharges
  the ROOT `a eq b` and says nothing about the RECURSIVE pointer-identity short circuits (`unionR`'s
  `a eq b` at every level, `IntTrieOps.unionTries`' `if a eq b then a` on whole child maps), so
  `S"a" u (S"a" <| {h0})` is ALREADY outside its own `touch` interval on pristine source.  That is a
  `priced` fix, recorded not attempted.

  tails-zipper — REFUTED, then landed with a one-line gate.  The new fact is `forced`:
  `execZ(s)` IS `materialize(transpileZ(s))` and `materialize`'s non-`Lit` arm is unconditional
  straight-line code, so AT DEPTH 0 the cursor is never a `Lit`, `children` and `terminal` both run,
  and `ZipperCost.tailsInter`'s standing objection — "`merged` is a LAZY VAL, a consumer that meets it
  with the empty space never queries it" — is true of an inner node and FALSE of the root.  All five
  zipper `tails` rows went green: `tails-union` `Work` 974.33 -> 21.48 and `Alloc` 74.00 -> 1.00 (an
  EXACT interval), `tails-inter` `Work` 1135.67 -> 35.86, `Alloc` 1344.00 -> 23.17, `Touch` 221.00 ->
  55.25.  The refutation was of the one LOWERED UPPER in the patch, which was not gated on `forced`:
  "every handed-through child is a `Lit`, taken by pointer" is a statement about the cursor
  `materialize` is CALLED ON, and at depth > 0 the consumer re-wraps them —
  `Intersection.children` is `ac.intersectionWith(bc, (_, x, y) => intersection(x, y))` and
  `intersection(Lit, Lit)` is not a `Lit`.  Measured on `Intersection(TailsUnion(a), b)` with both
  declared exactly: the collapsed `alloc` upper is a CONSTANT 2 against a counted `2n + 1` — 5, 7, 9,
  … 17 for n = 2..8, a missing Theta(nodes) term and not a constant slip.  Gating the collapse on
  `forced` too restores containment at every n.

  tails-work — SOUND, and it FIXED 36 PRE-EXISTING UNSOUND CORPUS POINTS.  `IntTrie.meetAll` opens
  with `if anyEmptyOperand(ts) then ...`, and `anyEmptyOperand` emits up to ONE PROBE PER OPERAND
  above everything `naryProbes` models.  At `k >= 3` the `Sigma|live|` ceiling hides it; at `k = 2` it
  does not, because `naryProbes` yields 1 there — counted on a two-headed `TailsIntersection`,
  `NaryOperandProbe = 3` and total `Work = 5` against a predicted `[2, 3]`, i.e. the UPPER endpoint
  BELOW the run.  `naryPreScan(k) = k` closes it.  CORPUS CONTAINMENT 80 -> 44 OF 3000, with
  `trie Work` 88.5% -> 100% and `graph Work` 96.5% -> 100%: the number that had not moved in this
  whole engagement.  The tails `Work` rows also gained a deeper must side (the root call was all that
  was claimed before): trie/graph 33.94 -> 19.16 (union) and 30.71 (inter), still red against the
  SELECTION tier of 8.  Its NEGATIVE RESULT is worth as much: `perProbe(k) = dedup/k + 3` was
  re-derived per call from the three loops and is CORRECT and does not double-count, so the upper
  cannot move without a per-level arity fact the shape domain does not carry — instrumented
  `Sigma|live| = 228` against the model's 402 and an amortised `perProbe` of 4.16 against 7, whose
  product is exactly the 2.86 ratio observed.

WHAT WAS HELD, AND WHY — both are correct findings that cannot ship alone.

  raffination-zipper — REFUTED, held entire.  It established that the 700 upper is NOT
  `ZipperCost.raffine`: `demandDriven` is true, so `analyze` meets the per-operator sum (1472) against
  the whole-region demand price (700) and the demand price binds.  The fact that would move it is a
  REAL BUG FIX: `Layers.termsAt(d)` is an upper bound on the TERMINAL depth-`d` nodes and was computed
  as `min(K_d, E_d)`, but `E_d` is the paths that REACH depth `d`, so on a uniform-length value that
  reads `K_d` at every level ("every node of every level may be terminal") — `T = [1,4,16]` for a
  16-path 4-head depth-2 value whose real terminal layers are `[0,0,16]`.  The identity is
  `E_d - E_{d+1}` and its sound upper is `E_d.hi - E_{d+1}.lo`.  IT STILL CANNOT LAND: it removes the
  slack that was masking a pre-existing under-prediction in `SpatialDemand`'s `CRes` arm, and four
  corpus points move outside their intervals when it does (both programs `Restriction`-rooted over a
  `Composition`).  Its second piece, a `work >= 1` floor for `restriction`'s BUILD-TIME
  `prefixes.terminal` probe, is individually well argued and also costs one corpus point — and the
  reason is worth recording on its own: on that program the zipper `Work` LOWER endpoint is ALREADY
  EXACTLY EQUAL to the counted total (69 = 69), so no new floor can be added anywhere in the zipper
  model until the existing over-claim in that distribution is found.

  iteration — REFUTED, held entire, and the floor it needs is now refused for the THIRD time with the
  mechanism finally identified.  Its four claims include two `collectJoin` collapse arms (a
  `provablyEmpty` body or a single live operand really does make `joinAll` return before it allocates)
  and a set of floors from `liveDistinct`'s `max(4, 4k)` scratch.  The refuter broke the collapse:
  correct about the `joinAll`, wrong about the TOTAL, because one `SpaceZipper` `TailsUnion` node costs
  `execZ` SEVEN `Work` events while `ZipperCost.tailsUnion` prices that node's upper at FIVE, and the
  dropped `naryProbes` term was the only slack covering the two-event deficit — `[28, 32]` against a
  counted 34 on `TailsUnion(a.iter(h, t, t & b))`, with 16 new brackets outside the interval over a
  400-program 4-seed sweep.  Removing the collapse left ten corpus points outside `zipper Alloc`;
  gating the floors on `kd >= 3` left four — the SAME four the `naryScratchLo` note recorded the first
  time; zeroing the alloc floors removed the improvement entirely.  THE MECHANISM: the zipper reprices
  a loop with the TRIE model (`controlFlowFallback`), so a trie-side `alloc` floor lands in the
  zipper's total, and `execZ` can allocate strictly less than `evalI` for the same term because
  `materialize` hands a `Lit` back by pointer.  Any fourth attempt has to make the floor conditional
  on which backend the trie model is pricing FOR — a change to `SpatialCost.go`'s fallback, not to the
  transfer.

WIDTH GATE 24 -> 12, CORPUS CONTAINMENT 80 -> 44 OF 3000.  Remaining: `fixpoint` `Touch` x3 (346),
`iteration` x4, `raffination` zipper `Work` (140.20), and the four `tails` `Work` rows on trie and
graph (19.16 / 30.71) against a SELECTION tier of 8.  OP-2g is RETIRED (`execT`'s `Alloc` meets the
budget tier on every operator of the table) and four more entries lost subjects, each recording what
left and why.  Two entries now carry a measured NEGATIVE result rather than a to-do: the tails `Work`
upper and the raffination demand price.
