--- ROUND 6 ADDENDUM: the one row nobody's cluster owned, found by the completeness critic --------

COMPOSITION IS THE ONE RING OPERATION WITH NO `a eq b` TEST, AND THE FRONTIER ASSERTED ONE FOR EVERY
OP.  `SpatialFrontier.binary`'s first short circuit was `if shared then Some("a eq b")`, and `shared`
comes from `sameObject`, which is true of a repeated `Mention`/`Literal`/`Empty`.  For
`unionR`/`intersectionR`/`subtractionR`/`restrictionR`/`raffinationR` that is exactly right — each
opens with `if a eq b then …` because each is idempotent or self-annihilating on a shared operand
(`x u x = x`, `x n x = x`, `x \ x = 0`, `x <| x = x`, `x \| x = 0`).  `compositionR` does NOT and
cannot: `x . x` is not `x`, so the algebra descends and rebuilds.

Reading `shared` as a short circuit there produced "the root pair and nothing else" for a term the
run walks in full.  Measured on `Composition(c, c)` with `c` declared exactly:

    shared=true    descents=[1,1]  rebuilt=[0,0]  ->  Alloc [3,1]   Touch [6,1]
    shared=false   descents=[1,9]  rebuilt=[0,6]  ->  sound
    counted:                                          Alloc 3       Touch 6

INVERTED INTERVALS — the unmistakable signature of a must claim for work that did not happen.  On a
6000-term randomised sweep, 14 of the 15 out-of-interval trie/graph rows were terms containing
`X x X`, and every inversion in the sweep was one of them.  The fix is one clause,
`if shared && op != FrontierOp.Composition`; the `{ε}`-unit short circuit two lines below is
composition's real one and stays.  Corpus containment 44 -> 43 of 3000 on the 200-program sample.

NO CLUSTER OWNED IT because it is not a width row on the operator table — the fixture has no `X x X`
term — and each of the six analysts was scoped to a cluster of RED rows.  It was the completeness
critic's first question ("is there a shared cause across clusters a per-cluster attack could not
see") that surfaced it, together with the observation that ONE MISSING CHANNEL explains three
separate sites: composition's upper collapses because the model ASSERTS sharing where the executable
has none; the fixpoint's floor over-claims because `mustDescend` IGNORES sharing where the executable
has it (`unionR`'s per-level `a eq b`); and the fixpoint's upper is loose for the same reason
(`descents.hi`'s two-sided Patricia bound ignores `IntTrieOps.unionTries`' `if a eq b then a`).

WIDTH GATE 12, NO STALE LEDGER ENTRY, CORPUS CONTAINMENT 43 OF 3000 (from 80 at the start of the
round).  Every remaining point is on the ZIPPER: `Work` 96.0%, `Rounds` 88.0%, `Touch` 94.0%, with
trie, graph and reference all at 100% on all four components for the first time.
