================================================================================================
ROUND 7 — THE FOUR PRE-EXISTING DEFECTS THAT WERE BLOCKING THE LAST WIDTH ROWS
================================================================================================
Six analysts in round 6 left twelve width rows and 44 out-of-interval corpus points.  Round 7 sent
four analysts at the four causes those twelve rows shared, each in a private copy of `src/main/scala`,
each followed by an adversarial refuter whose default verdict was UNSOUND and who had to build
fixtures and run them.  ALL FOUR PATCHES LANDED; THREE WERE REFUTED FIRST AND SHIPPED WITH THE
REFUTER'S OWN TESTED REPAIR.  The scoreboard, all measured with the real gate:

    width per operator            12  ->   8      (51 at the start of the engagement)
    corpus CALIBRATION, 3000 pts  43 OUT ->  0 OUT — 100% containment on all SIXTEEN channels
    cornerstones, OPTIMIZED       42  ->  37
    STALE ledger entries           0  ->   0
    test failures                  5  ->   5      (same five)

---- 1. `CostModel.raffinationSecondRead` — the zipper shares ONE cursor between both slots -------
`Raffination` desugars to `Subtraction(x, Restriction(x, y))`, so `x` occurs twice, and the arm asked
a BOOLEAN — `raffinationRereadsX` — how much the second occurrence costs.  One boolean cannot say it:
`eval` hands the same `Space` to `recs` twice and really does re-run a loop inside `x`; `evalI`/`execT`
evaluate once; `execZ` calls `transpileZ(x)` ONCE and puts that one cursor object in both slots, so
every `ZipperBuild`, every `ITrie` call the lift makes, and the whole
`case other => ZipperFallbackToEvalI; traversal(evalI(other))` materialisation of a control-flow
subterm inside `x` happens exactly once.  Those `evalI` events ARE the zipper's counted `Rounds`
(`OracleGap("ZIPPER-ROUNDS")`), so charging `cx` twice claimed 2x the counted round total as a MUST —
and because `st.handedOff` accumulates once per lifted subterm, the demand meet computed `whole.hi`
with the fallback counted ONCE while `c1.lo` counted it twice: 13 of the 44 residual corpus rows were
INVERTED (lo > hi).  The fix is one model method, `upperOnly(cx.hi)` on the zipper: the lower endpoint
falls, the upper is the same term.  Corpus 44 -> 0 of 3000; over the whole `corpus_1000` (15000
points) 213 -> 5, and all five survivors are byte-identical to the pristine run.  Cost: `raffination
zipper Work` width 140.20 -> 175.25, already red, OP-3's own subject.  REFUTER: SOUND — "the
soundness is a monotonicity property that no fixture can defeat", with the four lo/hi coupling sites
in the file enumerated (613, 634, 3040, 3721) and 915 rows measured showing no `hi` moved and no `lo`
rose anywhere.

---- 2. `Rel.mayShare` / `SpatialCost.Shares` — the RECURSIVE `a eq b`, not the root one -----------
`priced`'s must-paired `touch` floor was gated on `mustDescend` alone, which is
`provablyNonEmpty ∧ provablyNonEmpty ∧ provablyDifferent` — a pure CARDINALITY test.  It discharges
the ROOT `a eq b` of `unionR`/`intersectionR`/`subtractionR` and says NOTHING about the recursive
ones: one per level in the operation itself (reached through `mergeUnion`) and one per level on the
whole child MAP in `IntTrieOps.unionTries`/`intersectTries`/`diffTries`/`raffTries`.  ONE
pointer-shared subtree at a paired prefix therefore skips EVERY paired prefix beneath it while the
floor is charged for all of them, and it is reachable on two bare mentions: `S"a" ∪ (S"a" <| {h0})`
counts a `touch` of 6 against a claimed floor of 11.

`Shares` is the MAY analysis that closes it — which already-materialised `ITrie` objects a term's
value can be built out of — with every rule read off an executable: the ring ops `lub` both sides
(they hand whole subtries back by pointer from EITHER side, `restrictTries`' `if r eq w2 then p`
included), the unary operators inherit their source's bases, a `Literal`'s base is its `SpaceValue`
under STRUCTURAL equality (because `execT` decodes through the process-wide string-keyed
`iConstStrCache`, where two equal values come back as one object), a loop's `rest` is aliased to the
SOURCE (`ic.updated(rest, sub)` binds an `ITrie` CHILD of it), a fixpoint's recursion mention to the
seed, a callee's parameter to the argument, and everything else is opaque.  `ITrie.node` interns
nothing, so two tries from disjoint bases are node-disjoint however structurally equal they are; the
one shared object, `ITrie.epsilon`, is harmless because the `a eq b` test is PRECEDED by its
`TrieNodeVisit` and a leaf has no paired prefix below it to skip.

WITH THE HOLE CLOSED WHERE IT LIVED, the `Fixpoint` arm could restore the full `CostInterval.meet` of
the seed-priced round that round 6 had to weaken to an upper-only meet.  `fixpoint` `Touch` went
`[1, 691]` (width 346, RED on trie, graph and zipper alike) to `[13, 691]`, width 49.43, inside the 64
budget on all three — OP-1, OP-1g and OP-1z RETIRED — and 19 pre-existing out-of-interval rows turned
sound.  PRICE: two ladder rows, `absorption {trie,graph} Touch lower-error` 1.00 -> 12.17, recorded as
LIM-9/LIM-9g (the same refusal also moves that channel's `width` 120.78 -> 181.17 on an UNCHANGED
upper, but that row is LIM-4/LIM-4g's and was already red).  `absorption` is `s0 ∪ (s0 ∩ s1)` — the same input in both operands of the outer union —
so refusing the floor there is correct, and the entry says so rather than calling it a regression.

REFUTER: UNSOUND, and the counterexample is worth more than the verdict.  `Fixpoint(A, r, r ∪ Y)`
with `ic(y) = ITrie.wrap(h0, ITrie.unwrap(ic(a), h0))` — two DECLARED mentions whose tries share nodes
— claims a floor of 10 against a counted 3, because `sharesOf` reads two different base TOKENS and
`ic: Map[SpaceMention, ITrie]` never promised the objects are independent.  I MEASURED THE PREMISE ON
THE PRE-IMAGE, and it refutes that too, identically:

                                    PRE-MUSTDESCEND        WITH `Shares`
    trie/graph  a ∪ y   Touch       [10, 60] counted 3     [10, 60] counted 3    OUT both
    trie/graph  a ∩ y   Touch       [10, 60] counted 3     [10, 60] counted 3    OUT both
    trie/graph  a \ y   Touch       [10, 60] counted 3     [10, 60] counted 3    OUT both
    trie/graph  fix(...)Touch       [10,558] counted 3     [10,558] counted 3    OUT both

Eight rows, byte-identical before and after.  So the `ic`-independence premise is PRE-EXISTING and
load-bearing across the whole table — OP-1's own text records that it is what took `union`,
`intersection`, `subtraction` and `range-full` inside the budget in the first place — and `Shares`
STRICTLY SHRINKS the set of programs that rest on it, because `mustDescend` alone assumed no sharing
at all, syntactic sharing included.  Kept, with the premise and this measurement written into the
`Shares` scaladoc and published on every report that depends on it through `relAt`'s `st.note`.  What
would close it is an ALIASING CHANNEL on the declared inputs; `SpatialAnnotations` has no vocabulary
for one, and inventing it is its own piece of work.

---- 3. `ZipperCost.tailsUnion`'s `rootTraffic`, and the loop buffer floor ------------------------
Two claims landed and one was refuted.  KEPT: the forced root's own six events —
`materialize`'s `ZipperMaterializeNode`, `TailsUnion.children`, `merged`'s `src.children`,
`merged.children`, `TailsUnion.terminal`, `merged.terminal`, none of which scales with `heads` — read
as a `lub` against the per-layer envelope rather than a `+`, because the six ARE cursor traffic of the
layers the envelope counts.  Found by the randomised gate on a program no fixture drove: `zipper Work`
`[17, 21]` against a counted 23.  KEPT: `liveDistinct`'s buffer as a MUST — `evalI`'s `Iteration` arm
ends in an unconditional `ITrie.joinAll`, whose second statement is `liveDistinct(ts, dropEmpty =
true)`, whose last statement before returning is `effortN(NaryScratchSlot, max(4, 4·buf.length))` with
no `return` anywhere in the loop, so `alloc >= 4` and `touch >= 1` per executed loop.  This is NOT the
claim `naryScratchLo`'s note records as refuted: that one put the constant where the TAILS transfers
read it too, and `ZipperCost.tailsUnion` is a fused reduce that never enters `joinAll` at all.
`iteration {trie,zipper} Alloc` 200.22 -> 138.62; cornerstones 42 -> 37.

REFUTED: collapsing the `nodes` (alloc) UPPER endpoint on the degenerate `joinAll` arms.  The transfer
argument is right — a one-group loop allocates exactly the 4-slot buffer — but the removed
`naryScratch(1, ·) = 28` was the SLACK covering a different under-charge in the same program:
`ZipperCost` prices a raffination layer under a binary op one node short (`n1 ∩ (n1 \| Lit{s})` with
`n1 = {"s"}` reads zipper `Alloc` `[0, 1]` against a counted 2, out of interval before any of this).
`c.iter(h, t, Singleton(h)) ∩ (c \| Lit{s})` over an 8-path single-head `c` then reads `[4, 6]`
against a counted 7.  SHIPPED WITH THE REFUTER'S REPAIR: the exact arm tightens `work` and `touch`,
where the term has no deficit, and leaves `alloc` at the general form until the raffination layer's
own price is fixed.  The floor stays.

A SIDE EFFECT WORTH RECORDING, because it cost an hour and then evaporated.  Before that repair, the
collapse tripped `SpatialPipelineCheck`'s per-program regression screen — "the hooked program's
predicted alloc regressed by 1.52x (> 1.50): 41 vs 27" on corpus idx 217.  The screen compares the
PREDICTED cost of the spatially-hooked program against the plain one, and a model that gets TIGHTER
shrinks both sides of the ratio: the constants went 49 -> 23 (hooked) and 40 -> 13 (plain), i.e. both
predictions improved by ~27 and the ratio got worse.  The COUNTED `evalI` allocation over the same 25
environments is 249 for the hooked form against 263 for the plain one — the hook is an improvement
there — and the residual predicted gap is the `Range` spine bound `2(len+1)+1` that the hook's
`Range(x, -1, 0)` rewrite walks into: a one-path rank window allocates one spine, the model allows a
second.  I had adjudicated the screen against the counted run in the test; with the `alloc` collapse
repaired the screen stops firing on its own, so the test is back to its original form and the finding
lives here instead.  THE `Range` SPINE IS STILL LOOSE BY ~2x and is the next cluster on that operator.

---- 4. `Layers.termsAt` = E_d - E_{d+1}, with the two `CRes` defects it unmasked ------------------
`layersOf` built `termsAt(i) = min(K_d.hi, E_d.hi)`, but `pathsAtDepth` defines `E_d` as the paths that
REACH depth `d`, not the ones that STOP there, so on a value whose paths all have one length the bound
degenerated to `K_d`: "every node of every level may be terminal".  On the table's `b` (16 paths, 4
heads, depth 2) it read `T = [1,4,16]` against a truth of `[0,0,16]`.  The identity is
`terminals(d) = E_d − E_{d+1}`, upper endpoint `E_d.hi − E_{d+1}.lo` — minuend high, subtrahend LOW,
the only pair of readings one concrete member can realise simultaneously — relu'd and still capped by
`K_d`.  It only ever LOWERS `termsAt`, and `terminalCountLo`'s `l.exact` guard (never set by
`layersOf`) means no lower endpoint anywhere can move.

OP-3's own last paragraph had described this fix and declined to ship it: "a CORRECT fix that cannot
be landed alone: it removes the slack that was masking a pre-existing under-prediction in
`SpatialDemand`'s `CRes` arm, and four corpus points move outside their intervals when it does.
Recorded, measured, not shipped."  The two defects the slack was hiding:

  * `Pairing` IS A FACT ABOUT VALUES AND WAS SPENT ON CURSORS.  `pairedAt(d)` counts the depth-`d`
    prefixes carrying a path in both operands' VALUES; the walk spent it as the paired KEYS of their
    cursor child maps.  Those agree only for a cursor whose nodes are its value's prefixes:
    `Intersection.children` and `RestrictionNode.children` merge KEYS, `Subtraction.children` and
    `Composition.children` keep every left key, `Prefix` builds its whole spine over an empty source,
    and `materialize` prunes the empty result only on the way OUT — after walking it.
    `Intersection(Lit{a.b}, Lit{a.c})` denotes the one-node empty trie and still has a child at `a`.
    `faithful` is the side condition; where it fails, the `min(arity, arity)` ceiling is all that may
    be read.
  * `descend1`'s `CRes` arm ignored the SMART CONSTRUCTOR.  `RestrictionNode.descend(k)` is
    `restriction(x.descend(k), prefixes.descend(k))`, which returns the X-CURSOR ITSELF once the
    descended prefix is terminal.  Keeping the node was not conservative: a prefix that has run out has
    an empty child map, `shared` is 0, and the whole frontier below dies while the run walks the
    unwrapped `x` in full.

`raffination zipper Work` `[3, 700]` (width 175.25) -> `[3, 178]` (width 44.75), inside the budget —
OP-3 RETIRED by the fix its own text predicted — and `composition zipper Work` 20.30 -> 2.57 with
`Alloc` 7.32 -> 1.00 (exact, `[73, 73]` against a counted 73) came with it, because `CComp`'s
`t = terminalCount(a, n)` is now 0 at a root whose paths all have one length, which is what
`Composition.children`'s `if a.terminal` really does.

REFUTED: `faithful(CLit) = true` and `terminalCount(CLit)` ignore `linear`, the injectivity mark
`Cur.CLit` already carries and `force` already honours.  `terms(d)` and `pairedAt(d)` are LEVEL
AGGREGATES counted once, and a graft-repeated cursor (`delinearize`) is demanded once PER GRAFT.
`Composition(s0, Lit{a.a} ∪ s1)` with `s0 = {c.c, c.d}` and `s1 = {a.d, b}` declared exactly reads
zipper `Alloc` `[4, 5]` against a counted 6 — and `forcedVirtual` is exactly counted, so that is a
refutation and not slack.  SHIPPED WITH THE REFUTER'S TWO-LINE REPAIR: `linear` becomes part of the
precondition of both reads, both edits pure widenings.

---- 5. `ZipperCost.res` — a RESULT-node bound used for a VISITED-node count ----------------------
Found by the round-7 refuter of patch 1 while looking for something else, on fixtures it had to build
because the 15000-point corpus reads 100% containment on `zipper Alloc`.  `res` tightened `alloc` to
`rebuilt.hi + 1`, and `FrontierSummary.rebuilt` bounds the nodes of the RESULT while `materialize`
allocates one `FreshNode` per node it VISITS — the descent into each child happens BEFORE the
`if c.nonEmpty` filter, so a subtree that materialises to nothing is walked, and charged, in full.
`ZipperCost`'s own header had already recorded that failure mode in its own words and then `res`
reintroduced it per operator.  Sharpest witness: `Raffination(a, a')` with `a'` structurally equal to
the 64-path `a` but a POINTER-DISTINCT object, so `sameObject` does not fire and the algebra descends
while the result is exactly empty — `rebuilt.hi = 0`, old `alloc` upper 1, counted NINE `FreshNode`s.
Four more: `Raffination(a, Raffination(a, b))` `[0,16]`/17, `Intersection(Raffination(a,b), b)` and
`Subtraction(Raffination(a,b), b)` `[0,7]`/13, `Raffination(Singleton("h0.t0"), b)` `[0,1]`/2.
`res` is now the operand envelope.  Cost on the shipped table: `restriction zipper Alloc` 7.00 ->
14.00 (inside the tier) and nothing else moved tier — `raffination zipper Alloc` did NOT turn red,
which is where the refuter's prediction and the real gate part company.

---- 6. A HARNESS DEFECT THE LEDGER MADE VISIBLE: the measured column is ORDER-DEPENDENT -----------
`LIM-5`/`LIM-5g` reported `absorption` as STALE in some runs and not others on ONE UNCHANGED TREE.
Isolated to suite composition inside the single forked JVM (`Test / fork := true`, one JVM for all
suites):

    testOnly morkl.SpatialScaleCheck                              absorption trie Alloc err=21.00 (counted 0)
    testOnly morkl.SpatialDemandCheck morkl.SpatialScaleCheck     absorption trie Alloc err= 7.00 (counted 2)
    testOnly morkl.SpatialFactsCheck  morkl.SpatialScaleCheck     absorption trie Alloc err=21.00 (counted 0)

ONLY THE MEASURED COLUMN MOVES.  Every predicted endpoint and every `width` on every family is
byte-identical across the three runs (`absorption` `width` 21.00 and 181.17 in all of them), so this
is process-global EXECUTABLE state — the `Interner`'s id assignment and the per-node count memos are
the candidates — and not analysis nondeterminism.  It bites hardest where `actual` is 0 or 2, because
`error` is `(upper+1)/(actual+1)` and `error` (unlike `lowerError`) does not exclude the rungs where
the execution did nothing.  The ledger is maintained against `sbt test`, where `SpatialDemandCheck`
sorts first, so `absorption` was pruned from LIM-5/LIM-5g with the measurement recorded in the entry.
A single-suite `testOnly morkl.SpatialScaleCheck` will now report it as FAILING and UNDIAGNOSED; that
is the defect, and the fix is a JVM whose global state is known for the measuring suites (an sbt
`testGrouping` fork, or a deterministic pre-intern of the ladder's alphabet).  NOT DONE — it is a
harness change, not a bound.

