---- PHASE 0, CONTINUED: THE LEAN SCAFFOLD, AND A TOOLCHAIN RISK THAT RESOLVED TO THE HARD BRANCH ---

`proofs/lean` exists and `lake build` is green inside `sbt check` (`scripts/check_lean.sh`, one of the
twelve gates `scripts/gates.py` now holds).  Lean is pinned to `leanprover/lean4:v4.33.1` — the
toolchain elan actually has — and Mathlib to its `v4.33.1` release tag, so `lake update` reported
"toolchain not updated; already up-to-date" and no second toolchain was downloaded.  Mathlib's
`master` is on `v4.34.0-rc2`, so an unpinned `require` would have pulled a package needing a Lean
this machine does not have.  528 build jobs, ~1 s warm from `lake exe cache get`.

THE FIRST THEOREM IS `Zippy.Space.unwrap_wrap`, and it was chosen to make one point:
`#print axioms` reports `[propext, Quot.sound]` — Lean's own, no `sorryAx`, not even
`Classical.choice`.  Its TPTP twin `wrap_roundtrip.p` is `PROVED-MODULO T1` because it needs left
cancellation of append, which is asserted in `_cancel.p`, discharged by `mon_cancel.p`, and proved
from the induction SCHEMA `_path_induction.p` — a principle first-order logic cannot state.  In Lean
it is `List.append_cancel_left`, a core theorem from the recursor.  That is the whole argument for
the directory, made once on the smallest statement that carries it.

THE `% MECHANIZED-IN:` MARKER IS DEFINED AND SELF-TESTED, AND ITS FIRST VERSION WAS TOO LENIENT.
`scripts/proof_closure.py` lifts a row from `PROVED-MODULO Tn` to unqualified `PROVED` only for a
marker `check_lean.sh` has WITNESSED (built, `sorry`-free, axioms printed) in
`target/lean-mechanized.tsv` — untracked, so a reader who has not run the gate inherits no lift.
The self-test caught a real defect immediately: attributing the lift to the whole ROW reported
`card_wrap  discharges T1,T7` off one marker naming the wrap-roundtrip theorem, when T7 is the
counting axiom module and that theorem says nothing about counting.  Attribution is now per FILE — a
marker discharges the entry its own file ASSERTS (`TRUSTED[T]["axiom"]`, or an entry whose `rows`
name the obligation) — and a marker anywhere else is reported as ORPHANED and fails `--check`.
Verified both ways: in `_path_induction.p` it lifts exactly T1 on `mon_cancel`, `wrap_roundtrip` and
`card_wrap`; in `_cancel.p` (which asserts no entry, only re-asserts a lemma) it is an orphan.

TOOLCHAIN RISK, ANSWERED — `scripts/check_lean.sh --probe-kruskal`.  plan.md names this as a risk to
verify before 2E.3 and it resolves to the HARDER branch:

  * HIGMAN'S LEMMA IS PRESENT: `Set.PartiallyWellOrderedOn.partiallyWellOrderedOn_sublistForall2`
    (Mathlib/Order/WellFoundedSet.lean:836), with `WellQuasiOrdered` and the Nash-Williams
    minimal-bad-sequence vocabulary around it.
  * KRUSKAL'S TREE THEOREM IS ABSENT.  Every `Kruskal` hit in Mathlib is KRUSKAL-KATONA — a
    shadow/set-family theorem with no bearing on tree embeddings — and `SubtreeEmbedding`,
    `treeEmbedding`, `minimalBad` and "minimal bad" match ZERO files.

So `docs/TRUSTED.md` T3 ("the whistle terminates") cannot be discharged by citation, and 2E.3 must
prove Kruskal from Higman as plan.md's fallback says.  That is a real addition to 2E.3's size and it
is known now rather than after the work starts, which is the point of verifying at task start.

