  * `substS_nil` — the empty substitution is the identity, over all 22 space and 5 path
    constructors.  This is `Subst.apply`'s `if mentions.isEmpty && paths.isEmpty then s` fast path,
    whose correctness every call site assumed.
  * `seq_ne_simul_gyx` — THE `g(y,x)` FACT, with both readings computed: simultaneous gives
    `Union(b, Wrap(a))`, sequential gives `Union(a, Wrap(a))`.  The two arguments collapse into one
    variable.
  * `substS_keeps_freeM` / `substP_keeps_freeM` — CAPTURE AVOIDANCE, total over every constructor and
    all three binding forms.  It rests on two lemmas, and the split is the whole content:
    `bindM_not_in_range` (the bound name is free in NO replacement — in the no-capture branch because
    that is exactly what the branch condition denies, in the capture branch by `FreshSupply.spec`)
    and `bindM_lookup_ne` (the binder disturbs no other name's replacement).  `FreshSupply`'s
    contract is used at exactly ONE point and the no-capture branch needs no supply at all: nothing
    about the naming POLICY enters — not the order, not a counter, not `~`.

TWO DELIBERATE DIFFERENCES FROM THE SCALA, both recorded in the file rather than assumed away.  (1)
At a capturing binder the Scala does TWO passes (rename, then substitute); the Lean merges the rename
into the map and does ONE, which is what makes it structurally recursive.  They agree, and 1E.2's
trace check is where that is DISCHARGED rather than argued.  (2) Fresh names are a PARAMETER carrying
its own spec, so the theorems hold for every supply and the Scala's `~m0` counter is one instance.

A SIDE CONDITION THE PARTIAL SEMANTICS SURFACED.  `Space.unwrap_wrap` needs `p.den ρ = some v` and the
first version omitted it and did not build: with `s.den = some a` and `p.den = none` the left side is
`none` and the right is `some a`.  That case is exactly a `groundedPP`/`groundedSP` path — a term
OUTSIDE the certified path algebra, T6's boundary.  The TPTP twin gets the condition for free by not
having grounded paths in its signature, so it is invisible there.

---- 1B.1: THE HANDOFF, AND A CORRECTION TO THE PLAN'S PREMISE --------------------------------------

WIRED.  `CostModel.eventsOf` is new, `profile` is DERIVED from it, and `Backends.handoff(formulas,
phase, events)` returns a retargeted instance whose `BackendProfile.claimsFloor` is false.  `go`'s
`controlFlowFallback` arm calls `handoff` instead of `Backends.of`, which previously returned a
singleton reporting `eventsOf == Trie` — `claimsFloor` was TRUE across exactly the handoff
`PricingTarget` was written to make false.  `handoff` THROWS for a pair it has no instance for rather
than degrading to `of`, so a second `controlFlowFallback` cannot silently restore the bug.

1B.1a CAUGHT A LATENT FALSEHOOD ON ITS FIRST RUN.  `PricingTarget.formulaFor` was pinned to `Trie`
for every model, so `ReferenceCost` — which prices `Set[PathValue]` and reads no field of the trie
profile — reported `handedOff`, and every reference-side floor would have been withdrawn.  The old
code hid it by pinning `eventsOf` to `Trie` as well, which made `claimsFloor` true for all four
models including the one describing a different data structure: wrong in the permissive direction.
`formulaFor` is now the model's own `backend`, and the field's doc says why that is not the same
question as which REPRESENTATION the formulas read.

AND THEN THE PART THE PLAN GOT WRONG.  1B.1 says "every must-count checks `claimsFloor`", and
`PricingTarget`'s note justifies withholding the floor thus: "the zipper's total also contains
`materialize`, which hands a `Lit` back by pointer, so `execZ` can allocate strictly less than
`evalI` for the same term".  That sentence is TRUE and it is about a DIFFERENT term — a FUSED one.
`Unwrap(Lit, p)` is pure navigation, `materialize` returns the existing subtrie, and `execZ`
allocates nothing where `evalI` builds a trie.  It says nothing about a HANDED-OFF subterm.

Implementing the blanket withdrawal anyway was MEASURED to cost 15x-826x of interval WIDTH on the
zipper rows, for no soundness gain (containment was 100% before and after):

    CS-10z  width   884.89 -> 731 363.00      (826x)
    LIM-6az width   178 049 -> 8.01e+06       (45x)
    LIM-6z  width   10 025.89 -> 180 466.00   (18x)
    LIM-7z  width   1 821.44 -> 16 393.00     (9x)
    OP-9z   width   72.87 -> 1 093.00         (15x)
    CS-9    width   353.60 -> 26 537.00       (75x)
    CS-P3/4 width   2.02e53 -> 7.61e55, 2.67e53 -> 2.58e56

WHAT REPLACES IT IS A MEASURED PREMISE.  `transpileZ` (Zipper.scala) is a TOTAL, EAGER structural
walk with NO short-circuit — every arm recurses into every operand as a strict Scala argument — and
its fallback is `case other => effort(ZipperFallbackToEvalI); traversal(evalI(other))`.  So a
control-flow subterm is always REACHED and `evalI` is always run on it IN FULL, and every event
`evalI(T)` must emit lands in `execZ`'s counted total.  `ZipperCost.inheritsHandoffFloor` declares
that, carries the argument, and `BackendProfileCheck` 1B.1e MEASURES it on the three terms whose ROOT
is the control-flow node (so the handed-off subterm IS the program and the attribution is
unambiguous): `iteration` work 40->41 alloc 35->35 rounds 3->3 touch 15->15, `fixpoint` 8->9 / 1->1 /
2->2 / 9->9, `fold` 15->16 / 2->2 / 3->3 / 5->5.  `execZ >= evalI` on every component.

THE FIRST VERSION OF THAT MEASUREMENT WAS WRONG, AND PHASE 0 IS WHY IT WAS CAUGHT.  Run cold, `evalI`
then `execZ`, it reported `iteration alloc 47 -> 35` — an apparent floor VIOLATION.  It was the
process-wide decode memos (`iLiteralCache`/`iLiteralStrCache`/`iConstStrCache`): whichever executor
runs first pays the decode allocations.  `GlobalState.probe` is the list of exactly those tables, and
1B.1e now warms them and asserts order-stability in both directions before comparing.

WHAT WOULD RETIRE THE OVERRIDE: a short-circuit in `transpileZ` — an `Empty` fast path, a lazy
operand, a demand-driven transpile.  Then a control-flow subterm the ANALYSIS prices could go
unexecuted and its floor would describe a run that did not happen.  1B.1e fails on the first such
change, because it compares counted totals rather than reading the source.
====================================================================================================

---- PHASE 1, CONTINUED: 1E.3 — T1 AND T2 DISCHARGED; T7 IS A DIFFERENT KIND OF ENTRY -------------

THREE OF THE GATE'S FOUR ROWS ARE NOW UNQUALIFIED, and the fourth is honestly conditional on one
entry instead of two:

    mon_cancel       PROVED (MECHANIZED …#Zippy.path_induction discharges T1)
    wrap_roundtrip   PROVED (MECHANIZED …#Zippy.path_induction discharges T1)
    fixpoint_is_lfp  PROVED (MECHANIZED …#Zippy.Kleene.stationary_is_lfp discharges T2)
    card_wrap        PROVED-MODULO T7 (MECHANIZED …#Zippy.path_induction discharges T1)

T1 — `proofs/lean/Zippy/PathInduction.lean`.  The SCHEMA, for EVERY predicate, depending on NO
AXIOMS AT ALL (`#print axioms` reports none).  It reproduces `_path_induction.p`'s derivation rather
than shortcutting: `_paths.p`'s three freeness axioms as theorems, the schema generically in Phi,
both premises at the cancellation predicate (this corpus's `mon_cancel_base.p`/`mon_cancel_step.p`),
the instance stated verbatim, then `mon_cancel` DERIVED THROUGH IT.  `List.append_cancel_left` would
have proved the conclusion in one line; that was declined on purpose, because
`_path_induction.p`'s own header draws the distinction — the trusted item is the SCHEMA, which
"mentions `app` only inside the schema's two premises", and discharging only the conclusion would
leave it standing.

T2 — `proofs/lean/Zippy/Fixpoint.lean`.  The four bridging inductions, in the order the SMT file
asserts them (`chain_ascends`, `acc_eq_chain`, `init_subset_chain`, `chain_below_prefixpoint`), plus
`stationary_is_lfp`, which is the conclusion every lowering pass cites.  Both side conditions are
explicit parameters of every one of them, so none reads as unconditional — which is what the file's
own monotone-but-non-inflationary counterexample at :47-50 is about.  Part (iv) — the stationary
index EXISTS — is NOT discharged and is not one of the four: it is a `card` check whose bridging
principle lives in `no_infinite_descent.smt2`, and it is a well-foundedness claim needing a
finiteness hypothesis none of the four needs.  `stationary_is_lfp` takes the index as a HYPOTHESIS
for exactly that reason, so nobody can read termination out of a theorem that does not mention it.

T7 IS NOT LIKE THEM, AND THE GATE AS WRITTEN CANNOT BE MET.  1E.3's gate asks for `card_wrap`
unqualified too.  T1 and T2 are induction principles over free algebras: FOL cannot STATE T1 (it
quantifies over formulas) or DERIVE T2 (an unguarded `Int` index), and a dependently typed system
gets both from the inductive declaration — no model chosen, nothing narrowed.  T7 axiomatises an
UNINTERPRETED `card`, and `_card.p`'s own header makes the generalisation load-bearing: "nothing here
forces `card` to be the cardinality of a FINITE set … Every theorem below is therefore a fact about
ANY measure with these properties — which is exactly the class of facts tier-1 and tier-2
implement".  There is nothing to derive; proving the axioms means PICKING a `card`, and that makes
every counting theorem a statement about the choice instead of about any measure — strictly weaker
than what the corpus proves.

SO WHAT WAS DELIVERED INSTEAD IS A MODEL.  `proofs/lean/Zippy/Counting.lean`: finite sets of paths
with `Finset.card` satisfy all four counting axioms, the injective-image axiom and the comprehension,
and `card_wrap` holds in it.  That makes the residual `PROVED-MODULO T7` demonstrably NON-VACUOUS,
which is not a formality: `_nat.p` records that vampire REFUTES `_signature.p + _card.p` outright
when `card` is `$int`-valued, and `run.sh`'s guard against that is a vacuity probe — a negative check
with a 10 s budget.  A model is the positive statement and does not expire.

WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY CLOSE `card_wrap` is a TAXONOMY decision, not more proving: whether T7's four
counting axioms are DEFINITIONAL ("the clauses that say what the objects are … not assumptions that
could be false; they are the subject matter" — docs/TRUSTED.md's own line) rather than assumed.
They have a fair claim to it — they say what `card` IS, as `_paths.p`'s freeness axioms say what
`path` is, and `_paths.p` is not a T entry.  `Counting.lean` states the case and does NOT make the
call, because reclassifying the trusted base is not a proof obligation.

A BUG IN THE LIFT, CAUGHT BY `--check` ON THE NEXT LINE.  The first version of the annotator branched
on "was anything lifted?" and wrote `card_wrap` as `PROVED (MECHANIZED … discharges T1)` — UNQUALIFIED
while still reaching T7.  That is a row reported as unconditional while depending on the counting
axioms: precisely the defect `proof_closure.py` exists to prevent, arriving through the mechanism
meant to prevent it.  `--check` failed immediately, which is the point of having both halves.  The
fix is that `annotate` and `--check` now call ONE `want_verdict`, and a lifted-but-still-conditional
row composes both halves: `PROVED-MODULO T7 (MECHANIZED … discharges T1)`.

AXIOM AUDIT, whole package: 57 theorems, 10 depending on no axiom at all, and the axioms used across
the package are `propext`, `Quot.sound`, `Classical.choice` — Lean's own and nothing else.
