WHAT TRACK C DELIVERED

1C.1/1C.2 — `Cert` (src/main/scala/SpatialCert.scala): an immutable, structurally-equal,
hash-consed PREFIX TRIE.  It replaces BOTH flat channels — `otherKeys: Option[Set[PathItem]]` (e) and
`headAtoms: Set[Int]` (f) — with one `cert: Cert` field, and `object HeadAtoms` is deleted.  The claim
is `L(shape) ⊆ L(cert)`, pure may, no counts.  Identity is a VALUE: `equals`/`hashCode` are
structural and `Cert.arena` is a weak-keyed cache whose clearing cannot change an answer — the
property an atom id into a process-wide table did not have, and shapes outlive their analysis.
`GlobalState`'s three `HeadAtoms` probes become one `Cert.arenaSize`.

1C.3 — all three ⊤-degrading sites plus `capDepth` and `mk`'s width spill go through one owner
(`Shape.certOf` / `Shape.spillCertOf`) that keeps SUB-STRUCTURE: `Shape.of` at depth ≤ 0 keeps the
literal's own sub-tries, `sub` out of budget keeps the left operand's whole language, `capWidth` and
`mk` keep each spilled head's sub-shape, and `Shape.under`/`Shape.certUnder` read them back.

1C.4 — `SpatialFrontier`'s relational walk descends its untracked summary frame with
`Shape.untrackedTailBound` (summary MEET certificate) instead of into a ⊤ `otherTail`, and the
`maxKeys` enumeration cliff is deleted in favour of `Cert.headsDisjoint`.

1C.5 — `SpatialConfig.certKeys` (4096) and `certDepth` (12), a two-rule widening, and every
degradation RECORDED ON THE VALUE (`Cert.Degradation.DepthCut` / `WidthFold`) so it survives the
analysis; `SpatialCost.refine` turns it into an assumption on the priced report.

1C.6/1C.7 — `SpatialCertCheck` (8 tests) and `SpatialCertBudgetCheck` (7 tests).  `Cert.costNote`
prices construction, lookup, intersection and retained memory into `FrontierSummary.notes`.

THREE BUGS THE NEW SUITES FOUND ON THEIR FIRST RUN, all in the tier itself:
  * `Cert.leq` compared only the LEFT side's keys, so a key the right side TRACKS and the left
    reaches through its `outside` was never checked — `leq({a/X, ?/T}, {ε, b/Y, *})` accepted with
    `{b.t}` in γ of the left and not the right.  The identical defect `Shape.leq`'s own note records.
  * the width budget counted KEYS, and the fold's output then violated the budget it had just
    enforced.  It counts DISTINCT SUB-TRIES, which is also what the recursive walk costs.
  * `Cert.of`'s normalisation swallowed the degradation record: a depth cut one level down produced a
    ⊤ child, `Bounded(⊤)` normalised to `Unbounded`, and the certificate came back claiming exactness.

AND TWO PUBLISHED LIMITATIONS ARE RETIRED, with equality assertions rather than envelopes so they
cannot come back inside a ceiling:
  * `SpatialFrontierCheck`'s growing-DEPTH family was a published LOG FACTOR ("the frontier model is
    exactly as good as the size-only bound here").  It is now EXACT at every point.
  * the key-disjoint depth ladder was exact to `MaxDepth + 1` and then `hi = 4d - 14`; the recorded
    ceiling constant `RebuiltRatioPastCap = 4` existed to catch that line steepening.  MEASURED over
    d = 1..16: `hi == d` at every row.  The constant is deleted and the test asserts equality.
  * `Shape.capDepth`'s "WHAT IS *NOT* DONE HERE … the depth half of the review's ambition is OPEN"
    note is replaced by what closed it, including why the ORDER survived this time (`keysExceed`
    compares the two TRIES rather than starting a shape-level `leq` on the two `otherTail`s, which is
    what turned six corpus shapes red in the earlier prototype).

Past `certDepth` the depth ladder over-predicts again — `hi = 2d + 1`, measured over
d = 13..24 — and `SpatialCertBudgetCheck` asserts that the GROWTH CLASS is unchanged there (linear in
d, bounded constant) rather than pretending the budget is free.  That is the honest form of 1C.6's
third requirement: a budget may cost precision, not asymptotics.
