WHAT THE CERTIFICATE TIER COST, AND THE FOUR REGRESSIONS THE EXISTING GATES CAUGHT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Swapping the carrier's certificate channel is what plan.md's Track C header warns about ("the domain
where a smaller change previously produced three γ-regressions").  Four regressions happened, all
caught by gates that already existed, and each is recorded with the measurement that found it because
each was in the "obviously safe" direction:

1. LATENCY, 45.9x.  The first version made `Shape.language` a `lazy val` computing the WHOLE-language
   certificate (`Shape.certOf`, a full recursive walk with a `Cert.meet` per node) and every per-node
   consumer read it — `certJoinL`, `certMeetL`, `keysExceed`, `sub`, `range`.  Once per shape INSTANCE
   is still once per lattice edge.  `SpatialAcceptance` 5c:

       puzzle15   infer 1068 ms   decorated 49046 ms   ratio 45.9x   (budget 12x)

   The fix is `Shape.langLevel` — the SAME claim one level deep, which is exactly what the old
   `possibleHeadsCert` gave at the same cost — plus dropping the `Shape.inter` that `Shape.under` had
   started running on every descent.  A weaker bound costs precision and never soundness here: a join
   of weaker bounds still admits both sides, a meet still admits what both admit, and `keysExceed`
   refusing more often only makes the order more incomplete.  After:

       puzzle15   infer 673 ms    decorated 4016 ms    ratio 5.97x

2. A CLOSED HEAD SET THAT IS NOT CLOSED.  `langLevel`'s first version read `cert.outside`, producing a
   one-level bound naming exactly the TRACKED heads and CLOSED — and the meet with the shape's own
   certificate then INTERSECTED the two name sets and deleted the spilled ones.  puzzle15's 16 cells
   became `HeadSetWithin` of 12 with `others.hi = 0`, i.e. `MaximumHeadCount(12)` for a 16-head value,
   and nqueens' inferred size collapsed to `[0, 0]` against a true 32.  `Closed` only when
   `others.hi == 0`; open buckets get `Unbounded`, which `Cert.of` canonicalises to ⊤, so the meet is
   the stored certificate unchanged — right and free.

3. THE UNTRACKED COUNT CAPPED BY THE WHOLE HEAD SET.  The flat certificate held `complete MINUS
   tracked`, so `capOthersCert` capping `others.hi` by its size was right; the trie names ALL heads,
   so the same cap over-counted by exactly the tracked ones and puzzle15's head count went 16 -> 28.
   `Cert.headBoundExcluding` subtracts them.

4. THE ORDER, TWICE, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS.
   * A certificate that admits everything was not spelled `Cert.top`: under `Unbounded` a named head
     mapped to ⊤ adds nothing, so `of(true, {b -> top}, Unbounded)` and `Cert.top` have the same
     language — but `Cert.leq(top, that)` is `false` by its `a.isTop` arm, and `leqStrong` refused a
     pair whose languages are EQUAL (`SpatialPipelineCheck`'s narrow-weakens gate, channel
     `shape:otherTail`).  `Cert.of` now drops ⊤ keys under `Unbounded`, recursively.
   * And `langLevel` is ⊤ below the head, so where the right-hand certificate carries SUB-STRUCTURE —
     the case the tier exists for — the order refused again.  `keysExceed` pays for the deep walk
     exactly there, gated on `Cert.hasSubStructure`.  `Cert.depth > 1` was the first predicate and it
     was wrong: `{b/{ε}}` has depth 1 because `epsOnly` is a leaf, and it is precisely the
     certificate `capDepth`'s collapse produces.

THE DIAGNOSTIC THAT MADE (4) TRACTABLE is now part of the gate: `SpatialPipelineCheck`'s
narrow-weakens assertion prints the failing CHANNEL (`Shape.LeqShapeWhy`) and the two certificates.  A
bare "not above it in the strong order" sent the first two attempts at the certificate comparison when
the channel was `otherTail` and `Cert.leq` was already returning `true`.
