====================================================================================================
PHASE 1, TRACK B — 1B.6: THE DECORATION IS MADE TO DOMINATE, AND `refine` STOPS RE-INFERRING
====================================================================================================
`SpatialCost.refine`'s decorated branch re-ran `SpatialTyping.infer` on every node of every cost
query, and its own note said why and where the fix belonged: "the decorated analysis is a
WHOLE-ROUTINE run under `SpatialConfig`'s budgets, so at an individual node it may have widened or
capped where an unbudgeted single-node `SpatialTyping.infer` would not … Making `SpatialAnalysis`
strong enough per node to drop the re-inference is the right long-run fix and belongs there, not here;
plan.md task 1B.6 records it."

WHAT LANDED.  `SpatialAnalysis.Recorder` now meets a fresh single-node inference into every recorded
type itself, budgeted by the new `SpatialConfig.strengthenQueries` (4000, and 0 in `cheap`).  Two
decisions make it affordable and sound:

  * ONCE PER POSITION, not per observation.  puzzle15 has 295 positions and 13725 observations, and
    `refine`'s version paid `FactBudget` (2000) per node per backend on top of that.
  * With an EMPTY environment.  A position is visited once per head group, so an inference computed
    under one group's bindings is not a bound for another's; with no environment every free mention is
    ⊤, which is a bound under EVERY binding.  It is weaker where the node reads a binder — and that is
    exactly where the compositional traversal is already strong — and equally strong on the closed
    subterms where the budgets actually cost something.
  * A BOTTOM from the meet keeps the compositional type and adds a note.  The two are sound bounds on
    the same value, so a contradiction is a disagreement to report (`SpatialAnalysisCheck`'s
    consistency gate), not a proof that the node is empty.

MEASURED: with the re-inference removed from `refine`'s decorated branch, every one of the 32
(cornerstone, backend, component) numbers in ITEM 8's table is BIT-IDENTICAL to the run that kept it.
So the strengthening fully subsumes it and the cost model reads the decoration alone.

AND THE GATE, WHICH 1B.6 ASKED TO MOVE TO `improved == 8 of 8`.  8 of 8 rests on a premise that is
FALSE, and the measurement says so in two different ways:

  nqueens4/zipper                                   a POINT INTERVAL on every component: no room
                                                    between the endpoints, so no input type can move it
  nqueens4/trie, temperature/trie, temperature/zipper   the FRESH path already reaches the same bound

The fresh path is not blind — `refine` consults `histAt` on every node — so on a small closed term
whose inputs are declared exactly, both paths derive the same answer.  The decoration can only win
where a fresh per-node inference cannot reach: its LAW refinements and the BINDER refinements of the
whole-routine traversal.

So the gate is now `improved == informative`, where INFORMATIVE means the decoration is strictly
stronger than a fresh inference of the same subterm AT SOME NODE.  That is the direction the review's
objection is about — a decoration that carries more and changes no prediction — and it holds with no
slack: 4 informative rows (gol x2, aunt x2), 4 improved, 0 unexplained.  `improved >= 1` was the old
bar; it passed on one row out of eight and let the other seven rot.

NOT AT THE ROOT, and the first version of the criterion was wrong there: `SpatialAnalysis.of` already
meets an authoritative fresh inference into the root, so the root is never strictly stronger —
measured, 0 of 8.  The decoration's value is INTERIOR.
