====================================================================================================
PHASE 1, TRACK B — 1B.3: THE REST-CHAIN ACCUMULATE, PRICED PER LEVEL
====================================================================================================
`evalI` does not perform one n-ary join over every leaf of a rest-chained nest.  At level `d` it
performs one `joinAll` per depth-`(d-1)` prefix over that prefix's CHILDREN, so the joins are
`K_{d-1}` calls of arity `K_d / K_{d-1}`, and the operands at level `d` are the subtree unions below
rather than the whole leaf set.  The chain arm priced it as a single
`collectJoin(ch.leaves, ..., leafMeas)`, which is where `LIM-12`'s quadratic comes from: `naryProbes`
reads `k * (spineNodes + 1)` with `k = leaves` AND `spineNodes = leaves * nd(leaf)`, so both factors
grow with the rung.  Summed per level the total operand count is `Σ_d K_d`, which is what the
executor pays.

MEASURED on the ladder's own depth-2 nest at n = 64 (`Iteration(s0, h, t, Iteration(t, g, _,
Singleton(h·g)))` over 64 paths on 8 heads, so `K_1 = 8`, `K_2 = 64`), trie, definitional form:

    work    229784 -> 37648    (6.1x)
    alloc    43939 -> 17267    (2.5x)

and the level structure the arm now prices is printed in the report's assumptions:
`d=1 frames=1 arity=8 operand=8; d=2 frames=8 arity=8 operand=1`.

AND IT DOES NOT MOVE THE 33 UNDIAGNOSED `rest-chain/nest` ROWS, for a reason worth recording because
1B.3's premise is wrong about it.  `SpatialScaleCheck` measures `Routine.optimized`'s body, and the
optimizer HOISTS the loop-invariant head out of the inner body:

    given      S"s0".iter(h, t, S"t".iter(g, _, Singleton(h·g)))
    optimized  S"s0".iter(h, t, (P"h" x S"t".iter(g, _, Singleton(g))))

The outer body is then a `Composition`, not an `Iteration`, so `RestChain.recognize` does not see a
chain and the nest is priced by the GENERIC `Iteration` arm at both levels — which is already
per-level.  Verified directly: the optimized report carries ZERO rest-chain notes.  So `LIM-12`'s
attribution to `CostModel.chainNest` describes the DEFINITIONAL form, and the ladder's rows are
measured on a form that never reaches it.

WHAT 1B.3 THEREFORE IMPROVES is every chain-priced nest that the optimizer does not hoist — which is
the case whenever the inner body reads the level's own `rest` (puzzle15's 16-level nest is the
cornerstone example) — and the 33 undiagnosed rows are a DIFFERENT defect, diagnosed under 1B.7/1B.8
below.
