---- 1B.2  `TailsFacts.childKeySets` and the PKD descent price ----------------------------------
`TailsFacts.of` built `hs = kids.map(_.possibleHeads)` to decide `keyDisjoint` and then DISCARDED it,
so the key layout the n-ary descent price needs was derived and thrown away at the one site that had
it.  `childKeySets` keeps it, and `CostModel.pkdLiveTotal` bounds `Σ_calls |live|` PER OPERAND:
`2*m_i - 1` Steiner nodes in the merged Patricia trie (no unary internal nodes, so the binary-tree
bound applies) plus `carryDepth` ancestors, summing to `2N - k + k*carryDepth`.  Disjointness is what
makes it per-operand.  Met with the two aggregate arms, never replacing them.

    tails-union/inter  Alloc width   64.32 -> 22.21   OP-7, OP-7g RETIRED (budget 64)
    tails-inter zipper Work  width   92.89 -> 33.95   OP-7z RETIRED
    tails-inter        Work  width   89.60 -> 28.73   OP-6, OP-6g re-derived (budget 8, still red)
    tails-union        Work  width   56.02 -> 17.93

WHY THE LAYOUT-EXACT VERSION IS NOT SHIPPED, and it is a decision and not an omission: OP-6 records
the measured regularity it would capture (interleaved keys give `f(k)*m`, distant blocks `f(k)`), and
the difference is the DEPTH at which operands separate — a function of the interned `Int` keys, since
`IntTrieOps` branches on raw key bits.  Deriving it would make a cost prediction depend on
`Interner`'s process-wide, first-seen-order state, so a different program analysed earlier in the same
JVM would change the answer.  That is the fragility `HeadAtoms` was deleted for.

