----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1B.5: DISJOINT SLICES OF ONE OBJECT SHARE A BUDGET — 20 ORDERS ON puzzle15, AND THE RESIDUAL NAMED
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`|a x b| <= |a| * |b|` is the generic composition bound and it ignores where the factors came from.
When a composition chain's factors are `Unwrap(x, k_1) x … x Unwrap(x, k_n)` over the SAME `x` with
PAIRWISE DISTINCT CONSTANT keys — `Sliding.collapse`'s shape, one factor per cell of the board — the
factors are DISJOINT SLICES of one object, so `Σ_i |slice_i| <= |x|` and the product is maximised,
subject to that sum, at `(|x| / n)^n`.  Met with the plain product, so it can only tighten.

The keys must be CONSTANT: two `Deref`s of different refs can name the same item at run time, which
would make the slices overlap and the sum false.

TWO THINGS THE FIRST VERSION GOT WRONG, both found by measuring rather than by reading:

  * IT REQUIRED EVERY FACTOR TO BE A SLICE.  `collapse`'s arm is `(loc /\ b) x state(o_1) x … x
    state(o_n)` — the leftmost factor is a guard — so the chain was rejected outright.  The budget now
    applies to the slice SUBSET and the remaining factors keep their own sizes, which is sound because
    `|Π all| = |Π slices| * |Π others|` and each half is bounded independently.
  * IT READ THE SOURCE'S SIZE THROUGH `typeAt`, which goes through `shapeAt` and returns ⊤ the moment
    `State.budget` runs out — and puzzle15, the one term the law exists for, EXHAUSTS that budget
    ("spatial-fact budget (2000 queries) exhausted" is in its own report).  So it never fired on it.
    A `Mention` source's measure is already in the cost environment, put there by `loopEnv`, and
    reading it costs no budget at all.

MEASURED, puzzle15, trie, `Routine.optimized`:

    alloc   2.359e+52 -> 2.316e+35        touch   7.333e+52 -> 1.234e+36

and over the whole session's Track B work, from the figure this repository started at:

    alloc   7.610e+55 -> 2.316e+35        touch   2.581e+56 -> 1.234e+36     twenty orders

IT STILL DOES NOT MEET THE ITEM 5 CEILING (1e12) AND THE RESIDUAL IS ONE QUANTITY.  `(S/15)^15 <= 1e12`
needs `S <= 95`, where `S = |ass|` is the analysis' bound on ONE head group's tail-set and the truth is
16.  `loopEnv` bounds a `rest` mention by the WHOLE iteration source, and the two sound alternatives
are both worse here: the fiber TYPE's own size is 6.18e11, and `|src| - (groupsLo - 1)` is 2132
because the source has only two provable head groups.

WHAT `|ass| = 16` ACTUALLY NEEDS, stated so the gap is a domain question and not a TODO: `ass` is one
`act` group's tails of `superpose(q, tupleP).iter(l, t, all_moves(q).iter(act, map, act x map(l) x t))`,
i.e. `map(l) x t`, and the fact that makes it 16 is that a puzzle STATE MAPS EACH CELL TO EXACTLY ONE
TILE.  That is a FUNCTIONAL DEPENDENCY between two path positions.  The reduced product is
`Shape x SpaceType` — a per-head trie summary and a length histogram — and neither channel can express
a dependency between positions: the shape says which heads may occur and how many tails each may
have, never that the count is one BECAUSE of what the head is.  Adding it is a new relational tier,
not a refinement of an existing one, and it is outside what plan.md's Track C describes (whose
"retained per-head tails" is delivered and gated by `SpatialCertCheck`/`SpatialCertBudgetCheck`).

So ITEM 5's gate on puzzle15 stays red, `CS-P1..P4` carry it with their figures re-derived, and the
four laws this session added to the same term — `Shape.maxTailSize`, `Shape.tailsAlternative`, the
single-group exemption, and this slice budget — are each general, sound, and measured on their own.
