====================================================================================================
1D.1 HAS TWO CONSEQUENCES IN `SpatialAcceptance` 6a, AND ONE OF THEM RETIRES AN ASSERTION
====================================================================================================

`SpatialRecursion.summaryAt` is now the production consumer of the certified summaries, so a recursive
callee is no longer TOP.  Test 6a was written against the old behaviour in three places, and each one
had to be re-derived from a measurement rather than adjusted:

  (i)  the CARDINALITY is still unbounded without the law.  A transitive closure's size is not a
       function of its argument's LENGTH HISTOGRAM, so the summary cannot supply it.  Asserted.

  (ii) the LENGTH is now bounded without the law (<= 2).  Asserted, naming 1D.1.

  (iii) and therefore the block that asserted "the transfers alone cannot see" the emptiness of
        `closure INTER {length-3 literal}` is WRONG, because (ii) IS that block's whole premise.
        MEASURED, both sides: `empty=true`, the same single `eliminate-empty at /`, residual `Empty`,
        warm work `1`.  The law is not load-bearing for that residual any more.  The block now asserts
        the CONVERGENCE, and says so in a comment that names what replaced the law.

AND A GAP THE SAME MEASUREMENT EXPOSED, recorded because an unexamined absence is worse than a known
limitation.  The law's cardinality bound reaches the TYPE (`[3, 9]`), the FACTS
(`MaximumCardinality(9)`, `MaximumPathLength(2)`) and a BACKEND CANDIDATE (`TrieUnroll`, which does not
exist without it) — and it stops there.  The closure routine's `work` is

    UNBOUNDED(recursion in sn_tc: monotone accumulator, spatial parameter fixpoint failed ...)

WITH the law exactly as without it, because the cost model's recursion arm asks for a spatial-parameter
fixpoint on the accumulator and does not consult a discharged cardinality law about the RESULT.  Now
asserted in both directions (5b), so closing it is a visible `SpatialCost` change and not a surprise.

